     <div class="docs-meta"><span>Validation and evidence</span><span>Generated from the repository's scenario files and verification matrix at build time.</span></div>
     <div class="prose"><h1 id="capability-reference">Capability reference</h1>
-<p>The 61 capability cards from <code>web/capabilities.json</code>, then all 405 rows of the verification matrix.</p>
+<p>The 61 capability cards from <code>web/capabilities.json</code>, then all 412 rows of the verification matrix.</p>
 <h2 id="cards">Capability cards<a class="anchor" href="#cards" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
 <div class="ref-card"><h3>Orbit propagation &amp; constellation design</h3><div class="ref-meta"><span>Orbit &amp; geometry</span><span>Validated</span><span>666/666 AIAA vectors · 4.12 mm</span></div><p>SGP4/SDP4 propagation from real two-line elements (a committed Celestrak GPS snapshot) or synthetic Walker constellations whose mean elements realise the i:T/P/F formula to under 1 km over 24 h. Multi-constellation visibility, dilution of precision and GNSS availability, a constellation-design optimiser, streets-of-coverage sizing, and a Walker design sweep that reports the Pareto-optimal designs over a planes × satellites grid.</p><p><a href="/studio/?scenario=orbit-sgp4-gps&amp;tab=overview">Run orbit-sgp4-gps.toml in Kshana Studio</a></p></div>
 <div class="ref-card"><h3>Cowell propagator &amp; six-perturbation force model</h3><div class="ref-meta"><span>Numerical propagator</span><span>Modelled</span><span>conservative tiers &lt; 0.08 m / 24 h vs Orekit 12.2</span></div><p>A numerical (Cowell) propagator complementing the analytic SGP4/SDP4 path, with a hierarchical force model: two-body + the full J2–J6 zonal field, an optional EGM2008 tesseral geopotential to degree/order 70, epoch-driven Sun and Moon third-body gravity (a built-in ephemeris, no DE/SPK kernel), solar-radiation pressure with a conical umbra/penumbra shadow, atmospheric drag, the post-Newtonian Schwarzschild relativistic correction, and the Lense–Thirring frame-dragging term — driven by a choice of RK4 step-doubling or Dormand–Prince RK5(4) adaptive integrators.</p></div>
