e <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase (Cowell, Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon third bodies, the Moon from a DE440 cut) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectory of Artemis I over a 72 h coast arc from 2022-11-16 18:00 TDB, at 12 hour epochs, under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons target -1023 (the NASA navigation as-flown post-TLI trajectory), against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendment 1 changes only the request span). NOT MET and asserted as missed: 45.3 km largest position difference against 5 km, and 0.249 m/s velocity difference at 72 h against 0.05 m/s. A diagnostic written after the verdict places the miss in five impulsive velocity steps of 0.09 to 0.22 m/s in the reference (the pre-registered reading that the window held no burn was wrong); over the other 67 hours the model reproduces the next hourly state to a median of 0.38 m. Tests the mean state of one coast arc only: no covariance, filter, update or other phase</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise; propagator; mission_thread_oracle (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cold-atom interferometer error terms: Raman laser-phase noise, wavefront-curvature bias and fringe-ambiguity resolution</td><td>inertial::cai_error_terms (library functions, not yet reachable from a scenario kind): the Raman-phase weighting function of a square-pulse three-pulse interferometer (Cheinet et al. 2008, Eq. 8) with the white-phase closed form (pi/2)^2 S_phi0/tau_R and a numeric route for any spectrum; the laser frequency noise the retro-reflection delay does not cancel (Le Gouet et al. 2007, Eqs. 9 to 11); the pure-defocus wavefront bias sigma_v^2/R of a thermal cloud and its reduction by a detection radius; the wrong-fringe-index probability erfc(pi/(sqrt(2) s)) for a classical-accelerometer aid and for two interrogation times, and its inverse</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Each closed form against an independent numerical route in the tests (frequency-domain quadrature of the exact transfer function and delay filter, a Monte Carlo over atoms for the wavefront bias, a Monte Carlo over shots for the fringe index) and its limiting cases. One published worked value: Cheinet et al. 2008 (arXiv:physics/0510197, Sec. IV) state that at tau_R = 20 us a white Raman phase noise below -111 dB rad^2/Hz is needed for 1 mrad per shot; the white term alone gives 0.99 mrad (the paper derives the level with its Eq. 17, flicker plus white). Internal consistency and one worked value, not an external validation; higher-order aberrations and any specific laser's noise are not modelled</td><td><code>inertial::cai_error_terms</code></td></tr><tr><td>Greenhall and Riley exact equivalent degrees of freedom of the Allan, modified Allan and Hadamard variances</td><td>allan::edf_greenhall (with the edf_greenhall_adev, _mdev and _hdev wrappers): the full algorithm of Greenhall and Riley, Proc. 35th PTTI (2003), Section 3 (Eqns 7 to 13 and the large-J coefficients of Tables 1 to 3), for difference orders 1 to 3, noise exponents +2 to -4 wherever alpha + 2d &gt; 1, overlapping and non-overlapping, modified and unmodified estimators; NaN where it does not apply, including the unmodified white-PM case with ceil(M/S) &lt;= d, whose closed form is not implemented. assurance::uncertainty::edf now shares the code and keeps its outputs bit for bit</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.6 ci.edf_greenhall, a third-party implementation of the published algorithm, run by scripts/gen_greenhall_edf_ref.py over the grid pre-registered in xval/greenhall-edf/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 696f68e6, before any comparison): at all 4 876 points where it returns a finite edf, kshana agrees within a relative 6.0e-13 (bar 1e-9); at its 46 NotImplementedError points kshana returns NaN; every case label occurs; the wrappers equal the core bit for bit; uncertainty::edf is bit-identical over 274 280 values (pinned as a SHA-256 before the refactor). At 32 points (alpha -4, d 3, unmodified, case 2.2) allantools fails in its Table 2 lookup, so they have no oracle value and are not counted. Stable32 was not run. 0.36 revision (docs/revisions/STUDIO15-greenhall-edf-portable.md): on portable maths the kernel takes its logarithm from a double-double evaluation rounded once (allan::greenhall_ln), and the unchanged Bar 1 holds again at all 4 876 points, worst relative 6.0e-13; the uncertainty::edf guard is re-based to the 0.36 portable dump (3 746 of the 274 280 values moved, at most 1.6e-12 relative, from the host-maths base)</td><td><code>allan (edf_greenhall, greenhall_edf_core); assurance::uncertainty (edf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Oscillator holdover presets from public datasheets</td><td>Four presets — OCXO (Microchip OX-208), rubidium (Microchip 8040C), caesium (Microchip 5071A high-performance tube) and CSAC (Microchip SA.45s) — each carrying its datasheet's Allan-deviation maxima, aging and temperature bound; a non-negative least-squares white + flicker + random-walk frequency-noise fit scaled to envelope every datasheet point, a flicker floor never below the longest-τ figure, linear aging and a sinusoidal temperature term, synthesised as a seeded holdover after a GNSS loss</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The four named Microchip datasheets (document numbers and URLs in each preset and in docs/TELECOM-TIMING.md) for the input figures; the closed-form aging ramp and the fit's envelope property as internal checks. How the figures become a noise model — the fit, the floor rule, the 30-day month, the linear temperature reading — is a modelling choice; no preset is a measurement of a unit. 0.30 round 2, one-sided bars against measured units (pre-registered 43d19980), a finding (stays MODELLED): the IGS station THU2 Symmetricom 8040C in ESA MGEX final clocks has an Allan deviation 0.35 to 0.87 of the rubidium preset but a holdover error 1.07 to 1.60 of it; the 5071A record sits outside the caesium preset to 1000 s (measurement white phase noise, 30x at 1 s) and in holdover (1.15 to 8.74); the worst 2011 production SA.45s is 1.26x the CSAC preset at 1 s; the OX-208 has no measured record (blocked)</td><td><code>telecom_timing (PRESETS, fit_noise, with_flicker_floor, synthesize_holdover); models (ClockModel)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Holdover prediction from a measured clock record, checked on held-out data</td><td>The <code>slot-timing</code> kind's measured-record path: the overlapping Allan deviation of a phase record fitted by edf-weighted non-negative least squares in the white-phase and IEEE Std 1139 frequency-modulation basis (slot_timing::fit_weighted), then inverted for the coast time at which the time error reaches a threshold (slot_timing::slot_budget). The red-noise floor is measured from the record instead of assumed</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>A real free-running atomic clock: 556 990 one-second phase samples of a 5071A caesium standard measured against a hydrogen maser (A. Wallin, distributed with allantools; pinned commit and SHA-256 in scripts/fetch_cs5071a.sh), the same record tests/cs5071a_reference.rs uses for the estimators. The held-out two thirds are an external measurement the prediction never saw. Data-gated: the realdata-clock workflow fetches the record and runs the test with KSHANA_REQUIRE_REALDATA=1. Validates the fit-then-invert path on a white-FM-dominated atomic standard over coasts up to about 50 000 s; not the datasheet or class sources, not the deterministic terms. NOT a crystal oscillator: on a measured OCXO record (tests/slot_timing_ocxo_holdout.rs, allantools, 5.5 h against a hydrogen maser) the held-out prediction is conservative, at 0.59 to 0.70 of the measured breach, and mostly outside the bar, because that oscillator's noise floor halved during the record; in sample it is within 0.98 to 1.17. The crystal case stays MODELLED. NOT atomic clocks in orbit either: on 10 GPS Block IIF satellite clocks (tests/slot_timing_igs_holdout.rs, IGS final 30 s clocks, 14 days, protocol written before download) 8 land within the bar and 2 (G25, G30) are optimistic by up to about 2x, so under the protocol the orbital case stays MODELLED</td><td><code>slot_timing (ClockNoise::from_phase_record, fit_weighted, slot_budget, breach_after_sync_s)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Seconds until a free-running clock leaves a time-indexed slot's guard, and the fix cadence that keeps it inside</td><td>The <code>slot-timing</code> kind: the predicted error E(t) = k·√(σ₀² + σ_x²(t)) + (|y₀| + |c_T·ΔT|)·t + ½|D|t² inverted for the breach, the time left, the resynchronisation interval net of the fix latency and fixes per day, each term itemised with the dominant one named; clocks from the six ClockClass defaults (TCXO, OCXO and RAFS added, each citing one datasheet), the telecom-timing presets, an inline datasheet or a measured record; a breach beyond the source's longest averaging time flagged as extrapolated</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed forms for each term alone and the independent holdover::holdover_seconds root-find; the datasheet figures are the named documents (docs/SLOT-TIMING.md). A class default rests on an assumed red-noise floor and a datasheet source is an envelope of maxima, so neither is a measurement of any unit</td><td><code>slot_timing (predicted_error_s, breach_after_sync_s, terms_at, slot_budget, ClockNoise::from_datasheet, SlotTimingScenario); clock_state (ClockClass::Tcxo, Ocxo, Rafs, source)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Timing protection level for a receiver in orbit under GNSS spoofing</td><td>orbital_timing::orbital_timing, reachable as the <code>slot-timing</code> kind's spoofing section: circular-orbit speed and period, the longest time a ground spoofer can reach the satellite per pass, the clock-aided monitor floor and CUSUM detection latency, the conditional protection level (floor plus coast over the latency), the pull a spoofer at a stated maximum ramp rate accumulates before the satellite leaves its footprint or an independent check runs, and whether each ground-contact or crosslink check is independent of one ground spoofer</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Reduction to the existing timing protection level and the circular-orbit and spherical-Earth visibility closed forms. Conditional on detection, one terrestrial spoofer at a stated ramp rate, an overhead pass on a non-rotating Earth; not validated on a spoofed receiver in orbit</td><td><code>orbital_timing (orbital_timing, visibility_half_angle_rad, max_pass_s, crosslink_neighbour_is_outside_footprint)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Closed-form L-band signal power spectral densities and spectral separation coefficients</td><td>Unit-area power spectral densities of GPS L1 C/A and L2C (BPSK(1)), GPS L5 and Galileo E5a (BPSK(10)), sine-BOC(1,1) and Galileo E1 MBOC(6,1,1/11) (navsignal::Modulation::psd, with an MBOC variant added), their numerically located nulls and maxima (spectrum::psd_nulls_hz, psd_peak_hz, main_lobe_null_to_null_hz), and the spectral separation coefficient of a signal against any spectrum at any offset (navsignal::spectral_separation_coeff_offset) or against a tone, flat noise, a chirp or matched noise (spectrum::Jammer::ssc), with the anti-jam coefficient Q = 1/(R_c kappa)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Published textbook values: the BPSK(n) main lobe of 2n x 1.023 MHz null to null and the anti-jam coefficients Q = 1 for a narrowband (CW) jammer and Q = 1.5 for a spread-spectrum jammer matched to C/A (Kaplan &amp; Hegarty, Understanding GPS/GNSS, 3rd ed., section 9.4); the BOC(m,n) main lobes centred at plus or minus m x 1.023 MHz (Betz, Binary Offset Carrier Modulations for Radionavigation, NAVIGATION 48(4), 2001); the spectral separation coefficients -61.8, -64.8 and -67.8 dB/Hz for C/A with C/A, BOC(1,1) with BOC(1,1) and C/A with BOC(1,1) (Betz 2001; Hein et al., MBOC: The New Optimized Spreading Modulation Recommended for Galileo L1 OS and GPS L1C, Inside GNSS, May/June 2006), reproduced here from their Pa