levation of a satellite from a ground site, an azimuth/elevation mask table linearly interpolated in azimuth with wrap at 360 deg, the visible set el &gt;= max(mask_deg, mask(az)), and the common-clock DOP and ENU-and-clock cofactor matrix of that set (the kernel behind orbit::dop). It is how the [skyline] section decides visibility in the gnss-sim, jamming, leo-pvt, leo-pass and nmea-scenario kinds and, with the ground-site user_site, in orbit and integrity</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0): a TopocentricFrame on a WGS-84 OneAxisEllipsoid for the look angles and an ElevationMask per table, run by xval/orekit-skyline/OrekitSkyline.java on committed Earth-fixed states (the propagation is not compared), with gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 (wheel metadata MIT) get_dop for the DOP and the ENU-and-clock DOP matrix of the sets Orekit finds visible. Pre-registered (xval/orekit-skyline/PREREGISTRATION.md, commit d94130d4) before either oracle was run on the comparison inputs; Orekit's linear-with-wrap interpolation was confirmed beforehand on a synthetic probe. Scope: table masks, geodetic look angles, the masked visible set and its common-clock DOP and cofactor. NOT checked here: the canyon, sector and terrain reductions and the presets (their 360-point tables entered as inputs; rows 'Street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a table' and 'Skyline presets'), the per-system clock states and the error ellipse (row 'Horizontal error ellipse and per-system cofactor from the geometry covariance'), the GMST rotation of the user_site ground site in orbit/integrity and its dating of each propagator to one instant (Orekit is given Earth-fixed states), and any real obstruction (line of sight only)</td><td><code>skyline (Skyline::cut_deg, Skyline::visible); frames (look_angles, is_visible_skyline); orbit (UserPath, sky_view, cofactor_from_los); geometry (sky_geometry)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a table</td><td>skyline: the street