by lag-1 autocorrelation</span><code class="lg-mod">allan <wbr>(lag1_<wbr>noise_<wbr>id,<wbr> <wbr>lag1_<wbr>acf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>allan::lag1_noise_id: the Riley and Greenhall (2004) lag-1 autocorrelation noise identifier (decimated phase with its quadratic removed, or group-averaged frequency with its line removed; repeated first differences until delta = r1/(1+r1) &lt; 0.25 or d = dmax), returning the integer and unrounded power-law exponent alpha (+2 white PM to -2 random-walk FM) at any averaging factor; it sets the noise-type degrees of freedom of the measured clock library&#x27;s device cards</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>allantools 2024.6 autocorr_noise_id (A. Wallin and contributors, LGPL-3.0-or-later, run as a separate program by the fixture generator): an independent implementation of the published Riley-Greenhall algorithm, a uniquely defined quantity under the stated conventions. Validates the identifier, not the noise content of any real clock</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/clock_library_lag1_noise_id_allantools.rs (140 cases: ten Kasdin power-law records, b = 0 to -4, as phase and frequency data, af = 1 to 64; alpha_int and d identical, worst |d alpha| 9.6e-13 and |d rho| 4.8e-13…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="integrity"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Galileo OSNMA receiver-side navigation-data authentication</span><code class="lg-mod">osnma <wbr>(page,<wbr> <wbr>subframe,<wbr> <wbr>dsm,<wbr> <wbr>merkle,<wbr> <wbr>signature,<wbr> <wbr>tesla,<wbr> <wbr>mac,<wbr> <wbr>maclt,<wbr> <wbr>navdata,<wbr> <wbr>verifier,<wbr> <wbr>ubx,<wbr> <wbr>cli)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>osnma + <code>kshana osnma verify</code>: an open, pure-Rust receiver-side verifier for Galileo OSNMA (SIS ICD Issue 1.1, Receiver Guidelines Issue 1.3) from I/NAV pages or a u-blox UBX-RXM-SFRBX stream: Merkle-tree public keys (DSM-PKR), the ECDSA P-256/P-521 signature of the DSM-KROOT, TESLA key-chain verification (SHA-256 or SHA3-256), MACSEQ and tag verification (HMAC-SHA-256 or CMAC-AES) for ADKD 0, 4 and 12 with cross-authentication, chain end/revocation/renewal, public-key renewal and revocation, and the alert message; per-satellite status Authenticated/Failed/Unavailable with a reason for every failure or delay. Advisory: not type-approved navigation equipment and not a navigation integrity service; no anti-spoofing performance is claimed</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The official Galileo OSNMA test vectors published by the European GNSS Service Centre as an annex to the Receiver Guidelines (Test_vectors.zip, sha256 ef9b9afc6ef9e1c57393415cf2a1dc80c4035e7c06123907ea0bb97dd3ccf370): 18 of 18 sets, 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed, run offline on 2026-10-10 (evidence record in kshana-pro docs/evidence). Scope: the vectors validate the cryptographic verification (Merkle, KROOT/ECDSA, TESLA chain, MACK tags, ADKD 0/4/12) and its outcomes on the published scenarios; they use only HMAC-SHA-256, a SHA-256 chain, 128-bit keys, 40-bit tags and look-up entries 33 and 34, so CMAC-AES, SHA3-256 chains and other parameters are covered by published primitive known-answer tests and synthetic tests only. The trusted-time, data-binding, expiry and header-confirmation hardening is tested by the synthetic attack tests, not by an external oracle. Not checked: certificate revocation lists, live signals or a live receiver (the UBX word layout is checked on synthetic frames only), SHA3-256 Merkle trees, freshness without an independent time. Not an EU service</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/osnma_official_vectors.rs (opt-in, KSHANA_OSNMA_VECTORS: all 18 vector sets, 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed, per-set floors, chains announced, ADKD 0/4/12, alert verified, authentication resuming after…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Frequency stability characterisation</span><code class="lg-mod">allan</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Allan/modified/Hadamard deviation + power-law noise ID with χ² CIs</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NIST SP 1065 (Riley) / Stable32 reference deviations on NBS14</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/allan_reference.rs (NBS14 vs Stable32 to 1e-4); allan::tests</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Frequency stability on a real measured clock</span><code class="lg-mod">allan</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Overlapping Allan + overlapping Hadamard deviation on a real caesium standard</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Stable32 overlapping ADEV/HDEV on the measured 5071A caesium phase series (allantools)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/cs5071a_reference.rs (real 5071A Cs vs H-maser, 556 990 pts, 16 averaging factors vs Stable32 to 1e-3; data-gated via scripts/fetch_cs5071a.sh)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Allan estimator parity on the canonical Stable32 reference series</span><code class="lg-mod">allan</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Overlapping Allan + modified Allan + time deviation across the full AF ladder</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Stable32 reference deviations for PHASE.DAT (Riley; the standard regression series)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/phasedat_reference.rs (Stable32 PHASE.DAT, 139 averaging factors, OADEV/MDEV/TDEV to 1e-3; data-gated via scripts/fetch_phasedat.sh)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Extended-range frequency stability (Theo1 / TOTDEV)</span><code class="lg-mod">allan</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Theo1 and total deviation (TOTVAR) — extended-range long-tau stability estimators reaching ~75% of the record where the Allan deviation gives out near ~50%; the bias-removed ThêoH hybrid (allan::theoh_curve) built on them stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — theo1 (NIST SP 1065 eq 30) and totdev (NIST SP 1065 eq 25) on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG data set; regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/theo1_totvar/generate_theo1_totvar_reference.py</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/theo1_totvar_reference.rs (Theo1 + TOTDEV on the NIST SP 1065 §12.4 1000-point LCG data set, 6 + 6 averaging factors vs allantools 2024.06 to &lt;1e-9); allan::tests (white-FM closed-form tracking; TOTVAR=ADEV…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Maximum Time Interval Error (MTIE) — telecom wander metric</span><code class="lg-mod">allan <wbr>(mtie,<wbr> <wbr>mtie_<wbr>curve)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>MTIE(τ): the maximum peak-to-peak time-error swing over any sliding window of τ = m·tau0, the ITU-T G.810/G.823/G.8261 wander statistic synchronisation-network limits (MTIE masks) are written against — an extremal (max/min) figure distinct from the RMS Allan family</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — mtie() on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG phase series; MTIE is a pure max/min statistic, so the estimator output is bit-exact against allantools on the identically-built phase array (the committed 15-significant-figure reference constants sit within 1 ULP). Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mtie/generate_mtie_reference.py</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mtie_reference.rs (MTIE on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 1000-point LCG phase series, 9 averaging factors m=1..256 vs allantools 2024.06 mtie to &lt;1e-9, observed ≤4e-15); allan::tests (hand-derived peak-to-peak…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Modified Allan / Time deviation (MDEV / TDEV)</span><code class="lg-mod">allan <wbr>(modified_<wbr>adev,<wbr> <wbr>time_<wbr>deviation)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>MDEV(τ): the overlapping modified Allan deviation (second-difference sliding-window estimator that separates white- from flicker-phase noise), and TDEV(τ) = τ/√3·MDEV(τ), the ITU-T G.811/G.812/G.823 time-domain wander statistic the sync masks are written against</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — mdev() and tdev() on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG phase series (same series as the Theo1/TOTDEV/MTIE rows), computing the same uniquely-defined estimators; matched to &lt;1e-9 relative. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mdev_tdev/generate_mdev_tdev_reference.py. (The data-gated phasedat_reference.rs also checks these against Stable32 to 1e-3; this is the tight, always-on hermetic cross-check)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mdev_tdev_reference.rs (MDEV + TDEV on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 1000-point LCG phase series, 8 averaging factors m=1..200 vs allantools 2024.06 mdev/tdev to &lt;1e-9 relative, plus the τ/√3 identity on the…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Optical-clock frequency stability on a real measured curve</span><code class="lg-mod">quantum_<wbr>trade <wbr>(qparams_<wbr>from_<wbr>adev_<wbr>curve),<wbr> <wbr>powerlaw</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Power-law (white-FM + red-noise-floor) NNLS recovery from a published measured ⁸⁸Sr optical-clock-transition Allan deviation — reproducing σ_y(τ) and the headline 4.7e-16/√τ short-τ scaling with a genuine measured long-τ floor, rather than the synthesised optical-class floor holdover.rs otherwise assumes</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Norcia, Young, Eckner, Oelker, Ye, Kaufman, Science 366:93 (2019), Fig. 4 measured ADEV; curve vendored verbatim from Zenodo 10.5281/zenodo.3382347 (CC-BY-4.0). Scoped to reproducing the published measured stability curve — the clock-class holdover-to-threshold device figures stay MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/optical_clock_adev_reference.rs (Norcia et al. ⁸⁸Sr tweezer-clock σ_y(τ), 8 averaging times 0.92–117.76 s vendored verbatim under CC-BY-4.0: NNLS reconstructs the curve to ~10% RMS / ≤21% worst point; recovered…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Integrity (RAIM/ARAIM/SBAS)</span><code class="lg-mod">araim_<wbr>reference <wbr>(add_<wbr>v42_<wbr>protection_<wbr>levels,<wbr> <wbr>add_<wbr>v42_<wbr>protection_<wbr>levels_<wbr>ecef); <wbr>raim <wbr>(snapshot_<wbr>raim,<wbr> <wbr>snapshot_<wbr>raim_<wbr>fde,<wbr> <wbr>araim_<wbr>raim,<wbr> <wbr>araim_<wbr>dual_<wbr>raim,<wbr> <wbr>solution_<wbr>separation_<wbr>raim); <wbr>sbas <wbr>(sbas_<wbr>protection_<wbr>level); <wbr>lunar</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>ARAIM multiple-hypothesis solution separation per the ARAIM Airborne Design Document (ADD) v4.2 (araim_reference::add_v42_protection_levels and add_v42_protection_levels_ecef: subset determination by the P_THRES and FC_THRES rules, VPL, HPL, the effective monitor threshold EMT and sigma_acc); chi-squared snapshot RAIM fault detection with single-satellite exclusion (raim::snapshot_raim, raim::snapshot_raim_fde) and slope-based HPL/VPL on the geodetic local level; and the SBAS DO-229E protection-level combination (sbas::sbas_protection_level) on the L1 service. NOT covered, stated plainly: the uniform-sigma convenience functions raim::araim_raim and raim::araim_dual_raim are NOT ADD-conformant (on matched inputs they miss Stanford MAAST by 0.75 m VPL / 4.04 m HPL and 0.39 m VPL / 1.93 m HPL, a finding); the dual-frequency multi-constellation (DFMC) L5 SBAS comparison is a finding (308 user-epoch pairs with no GEO in view are protected by Kshana and not by MAAST, whose GEO-reception gate Kshana does not model); raim::solution_separation_raim has no external comparison</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Three independent tools, pre-registered (ba7a01a7) before any fixture or oracle output existed, tolerances fixed then. (1) ARAIM: Stanford MAAST for ARAIM 2 (commit ab70e2a3, BSD-3) mhss_raim_baseline_v5.m, run as a separate program under GNU Octave 8.4 with a driver-side unique() compatibility shim (disclosed, no algorithm change), at the ADD TOL_PL of 0.05 m: the Rust reproduces the ADD reference implementation on 270 real cases. Disclosed: the Kshana ADD path was written after the pre-registration, following the ADD as MAAST implements it; an independent hold-out by the reviewer (270 new user geometries and a second integrity support message, MAAST re-run against the frozen code) passed at the same bar. (2) Snapshot RAIM: RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 (BSD-2) estpos/valsol/raim_fde in a separate C harness for detection and exclusion; the slope PL from RTKLIB matinv/xyz2enu plus SciPy 1.18.1 chi2/ncx2, where both sides evaluate the same closed form (Brown; Parkinson and Axelrad), so the PL check covers the implementation, not the formula. (3) SBAS: Stanford MAAST (commit 7d32b049) usr_vhpl, unmodified, on MAAST own recorded WAAS broadcasts: L1 levels within 1e-4 m after the K rescaling and an equal protected set. The same comparisons produced the findings stated in the capability: the uniform-sigma ARAIM functions are not ADD-conformant, and the L5 set equality fails on 308 pairs with no GEO in view (the levels on the 3267 pairs both tools protect agree to 1e-13 m). The DO-229E/DO-316 K-factors are transcribed constants and the real geometry is an input; neither is counted as an oracle. 0.30 revision: snapshot_raim protection levels moved from the radial to the geodetic local level (up to 0.31 m HPL and 0.14 m VPL at 16 deg N)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/integrity_araim_stanford_oracle.rs::araim_mhss_matches_stanford_maast_add_v4_2 (270 real-geometry cases, Celestrak GPS+Galileo and IGS SP3: worst |dVPL| 2.2e-3 m, |dHPL| 1.1e-3 m, |dEMT| 2.1e-10 m against the ADD…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Orbit propagation &amp; determination</span><code class="lg-mod">sgp4,<wbr> <wbr>propagator,<wbr> <wbr>orbit_<wbr>determination,<wbr> <wbr>precise_<wbr>od</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>SGP4/SDP4, Cowell 6-DOF + perturbations, batch/sequential OD</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>AIAA 2006-6753 SGP4 verification vectors</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/sgp4_verification.rs (666 AIAA verification vectors, worst 4.12 mm); tests/sgp4_crate_comparison.rs (independent sgp4 crate)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Numerical Cowell propagator &amp; force model</span><code class="lg-mod">propagator,<wbr> <wbr>forces</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Cowell numerical propagator with a hierarchical force model (two-body, J2–J6 zonal, Sun/Moon third-body, cannonball SRP, exponential drag); RK4 step-doubling / DP5(4) / DOP853</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 — an independent library, a different integrator and spherical-harmonic recursion. The conservative tiers (two-body → J2–J6 zonal → Sun/Moon third-body → cannonball SRP) agree to sub-metre over a 24 h arc, validating the integrator + force algebra; the drag tier and the absolute Sun/Moon-ephemeris / density input fidelity stay MODELLED (characterisation only)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/numerical_cowell_propagator_reference.rs (275 epochs = 11 cases × 25 hourly states, LEO+GTO, vs Orekit 12.2 DormandPrince853; conservative tiers T1–T5 worst |Δr| 0.08 m over 24 h; drag tier characterised at 333 m)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Oblate-Earth SRP shadow (opt-in)</span><code class="lg-mod">srp_<wbr>shadow,<wbr> <wbr>forces <wbr>(srp_<wbr>accel_<wbr>with_<wbr>lit_<wbr>fraction),<wbr> <wbr>propagator</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>SRP lit fraction with the WGS-84 ellipsoid as the occulting body (apparent Earth radius from the ellipsoid limb towards the Sun, dual-disk lens formula; srp_shadow::oblate_conical_shadow), opt-in on the Cowell force model through propagator::ForceModel::srp_shadow (default: the spherical conical shadow, unchanged)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) SolarRadiationPressure.getLightingRatio with a WGS-84 and a spherical OneAxisEllipsoid occulting body, run as a separate program with the Sun fixed at the same inertial position. Bars pre-registered in xval/srp-oblate-shadow-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (c6b751e9): 1e-6 on both, and a &gt; 1e-2 oblate-vs-sphere spread; all met. The ellipsoid axis is the inertial z axis on both sides (no precession-nutation)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/srp_oblate_shadow_orekit_oracle.rs (2400 geometries across the shadow edge, 884 in penumbra, LEO to GEO, Sun declination 0 and +-23.44 deg, 8 azimuths: worst |dnu| 7.41e-9 for WGS-84 and 2.24e-9 for the spherical…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Box-wing and ECOM2 solar radiation pressure, with partials (opt-in)</span><code class="lg-mod">srp_<wbr>boxwing,<wbr> <wbr>propagator,<wbr> <wbr>precise_<wbr>od</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Non-cannonball SRP: a box-wing flat-plate model (Vallado eq. 8-44; single-sided body panels, sun-pointing wings) with the Galileo FOC, IOV and IOV end-of-life tables of the GSC satellite metadata, in nominal GNSS yaw steering, with analytic partials for the radiation factor and every panel&#x27;s specular and diffuse coefficients; and the ECOM2 empirical model (D2/B1) with its partials. The box-wing is opt-in on the Cowell propagator (propagator::ForceModel::box_wing_srp, setting its srp_boxwing field) and on the precise orbit-determination model (precise_od::PreciseForceModel::box_wing_srp), replacing the cannonball term with cr as the radiation factor; both default to None, leaving every existing path byte-identical. ECOM2 is not wired into a force model</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) BoxAndSolarArraySpacecraft with FixedPanel and PointingPanel, SolarRadiationPressure(dRef = 1 au, pRef = 1361/c), GenericGNSS nominal yaw and ECOM2(nD = 2, nB = 1), run as a separate program with the Sun at fixed inertial positions; the radiation-factor derivative from Hipparchus Gradient, per-panel derivatives as central differences of Orekit (Orekit has none). Bars pre-registered in xval/srp-boxwing-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (42e864bb), before any Orekit output existed: 1e-12 relative (1e-11 end to end, 1e-9 for the differences); all met (RESULTS.md). VALIDATED covers the model evaluation only: the GSC tables are transcribed inputs, and the adequacy of a box-wing for real Galileo orbits, thermal re-emission, the modified low-beta yaw law and eclipse turns are not covered; the Galileo SRP prediction stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/srp_boxwing_orekit_oracle.rs (252 Galileo-orbit geometries, 7 Sun cases including perihelion and aphelion distances, 100 random attitudes; flat-plate sum worst 2.05e-15 relative over 1056 cases; end to end with…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Solar limb-darkened SRP shadow (opt-in)</span><code class="lg-mod">srp_<wbr>shadow</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>SRP lit fraction as the un-occulted fraction of the limb-darkened solar flux (Eddington-approximation law I(mu)/I(1) = 2/5 + 3/5 mu, Gauss-Legendre quadrature) over a spherical or WGS-84 Earth (srp_shadow::limb_darkened_shadow, the ShadowModel variant OblateEarthLimbDarkened)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>None external: Orekit 12.2 has no limb-darkened lighting ratio. Checked against closed forms of the same law only</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>srp_shadow::tests (the annular closed form, the uniform law reproducing the conical shadow, full sun and umbra limits)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">NRLMSISE-00 thermospheric density</span><code class="lg-mod">nrlmsise00</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>A port of the NRLMSISE-00 empirical thermosphere (gtd7 and gtd7d: species densities, total mass density with anomalous oxygen, temperatures; the 24 switches; daily-Ap and 3-hourly ap-array modes) from the public-domain C version of the NRL Fortran</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pymsis 0.13.0 (MIT) version=0, which runs the NRL Fortran GTD7D in single precision, and the NRLMSISE-00 C port (release 20190709, public domain) compiled as a separate program, plus the C distribution&#x27;s 17 published test outputs. Bars pre-registered in xval/nrlmsise00-pymsis/PREREGISTRATION.md (a6504ec9) before the port existed; all met (RESULTS.md). Validates the port, not the model&#x27;s accuracy against measured density</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/nrlmsise00_reference.rs (720 grid points, 90 to 1000 km, 4 latitudes, 2 local times, 3 days, low/moderate/high activity: density within 1.866e-6 relative and temperature within 1.97e-4 K of pymsis; bit-identical…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">NRLMSISE-00 drag-density tier (opt-in)</span><code class="lg-mod">forces <wbr>(density_<wbr>at,<wbr> <wbr>drag_<wbr>accel_<wbr>with_<wbr>density),<wbr> <wbr>propagator</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>forces::DensityModel (Exponential default, Nrlmsise00 { f107a, f107, ap }) and propagator::ForceModel::drag_density: quadratic drag against the co-rotating atmosphere with the NRLMSISE-00 density at the WGS-84 geodetic point (Earth rotation angle with UT1 = TT, local solar time UT + lon/15), constant space weather per model; default off and bit-identical</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with DragForce over Orekit&#x27;s own NRLMSISE00 implementation, run as a separate program with the same Earth-rotation-angle frame, WGS-84 shape, local-solar-time convention (a mean Sun) and TT as UT; the convention setup was checked oracle against oracle (Orekit vs pymsis, 5.2e-5 in density). Bars pre-registered in xval/drag-tier-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (f380f42c): 0.5 m + 1e-3 of the drag displacement, and two &gt; 100 m sensitivity checks; all met. Not covered: the model&#x27;s accuracy against measured density, time-varying space weather, a GMAT leg</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/drag_tier_orekit_oracle.rs (4 cases x 25 hourly states over 24 h, circular 51.6 deg, 400 km at low/moderate/high activity and 300 km: worst |dr| 0.0303 m and |dv| 3.36e-5 m/s against 3.3 to 141.8 km of drag…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Default exponential drag against an independent implementation (equatorial)</span><code class="lg-mod">forces <wbr>(atmospheric_<wbr>density,<wbr> <wbr>drag_<wbr>accel),<wbr> <wbr>propagator</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The default drag of the Cowell propagator: quadratic drag against the co-rotating atmosphere with forces::atmospheric_density, the piecewise-exponential table of Vallado Table 8-4 at the spherical altitude (propagator::ForceModel::drag with the default Exponential variant of forces::DensityModel)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>GMAT R2022a (NASA GSFC, Apache-2.0) GmatConsole with the Exponential atmosphere (the same 28-band table), point-mass Earth and PrinceDormand78, run, never vendored. Bars pre-registered in xval/drag-exponential-gmat/PREREGISTRATION.md (d870eb71) on the drag displacement of each side (drag minus no-drag run), which cancels the two Earth mu values and integrators; all met (RESULTS.md). GMAT takes geodetic height and kshana spherical altitude: they coincide only on the equator, so VALIDATED covers equatorial orbits; off the equator the models differ by design (10.4 % of 24 h displacement at 51.6 deg, 400 km). GMAT&#x27;s public build has no NRLMSISE-00 (plugin absent), so the NRLMSISE-00 tier has the Orekit oracle only. The table&#x27;s accuracy against measured density is not covered</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/drag_exponential_gmat_oracle.rs (equatorial cases E400, E300 and E250x600 at J2000.0, 25 hourly states over 24 h: the drag-induced displacement agrees with GMAT&#x27;s to at most 0.016 of the bar 0.5 m + 1e-3|D|…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">DE440 Sun and Moon in the Cowell force model (opt-in)</span><code class="lg-mod">propagator <wbr>(ForceModel::<wbr>with_<wbr>ephemeris,<wbr> <wbr>ForceEphemeris),<wbr> <wbr>ephem_<wbr>provider</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Opt-in ephemeris tier for the third-body and SRP terms of the numerical Cowell ForceModel: the geocentric Sun and Moon from any EphemerisProvider (a DE440 SPK kernel through KernelEphemeris) at TDB = TT + (TDB - TT), refusing a provider that cannot serve the epoch and counting any fallback to the analytic series; default off</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with ThirdBodyAttraction and SolarRadiationPressure on Orekit&#x27;s own DE440 reader (orekit-data lnxp1990.440), run as a separate program; kshana reads a bit-for-bit, SPICE-checked cut of NAIF de440s.bsp. Bars pre-registered in xval/propagator-de440-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (6386b3a2). The orbit bar (0.5 m) and the sensitivity control are met, but the Sun/Moon position bar (3 m / 0.2 m) missed at 6.39 m / 0.244 m. SPICE reading the same kernel at its exact TDB misses Orekit by the same amount (6.25 m / 0.240 m), so the gap lies between NAIF&#x27;s de440s path and Orekit&#x27;s DE440 reader or time scale; cause not isolated. Recorded and pinned, so the row stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/propagator_de440_orekit_oracle.rs (5 cases x 25 hourly states over 24 h, LEO, GEO and HEO, third body and third body + SRP: worst |dr| 0.0170 m, |dv| 1.84e-5 m/s; analytic-series control misses by 9.96 m; Sun…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">S1 atmospheric tide (opt-in)</span><code class="lg-mod">tides,<wbr> <wbr>propagator,<wbr> <wbr>precise_<wbr>od</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The diurnal S1 air-pressure tide as a geopotential perturbation: Biancale and Bode (2006) model N1, degrees 2 to 6, through the IERS 2010 Eq. 6.21 load formula (tides::atmospheric_tide_s1, s1_air_tide_acceleration), opt-in on the Cowell and precise force models (air_tide_s1, default off)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>None external for the geopotential effect. The 24 coefficients are transcribed from GFZ STR06/01 Table 1 model N1 and were re-checked row by row against the report (SHA-256 890865ae); degrees 0, 1, 7 and 8 are not used. Ray and Ponte (2003) print no spherical-harmonic table, so no second source was compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tides::tests (the S1 term is diurnal, turns 90 deg in a quarter day, and has the load-factor amplitude of each row; physical magnitude at LEO); propagator::tests::the_s1_air_tide_flag_adds_exactly_the_s1_acceleration</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">S2 atmospheric tide coefficients in the IERS convention</span><code class="lg-mod">tides <wbr>(s2_<wbr>air_<wbr>waves,<wbr> <wbr>atmospheric_<wbr>tide)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Ray (2001) S2 air-pressure harmonics (n &lt;= 4, prograde and retrograde) converted to the fully-normalised, sine-lead form of IERS 2010 Eqs. 6.20-6.21 (amplitude divided by N_nm, epsilon = 90 deg - psi) and applied as the S2 term of tides::atmospheric_tide</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Biancale and Bode, GFZ Scientific Technical Report STR06/01 (2006), Table 5, model A2: Ray (2001) published in fully-normalised harmonics with sine-lead phases, an independent rendering of the same model. Pre-registered in xval/s2-air-tide-fix/PREREGISTRATION.md (d2c3d297) with the fix it validates. Covers the coefficient conversion only; the air tide&#x27;s geopotential effect has no external oracle</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/s2_air_tide_gfz_table5.rs (21 of 21 waves agree with the published model A2: worst 0.00050 cm, 0.050 deg against bars of 0.0006 cm, 0.1 deg); tests/tides_iers.rs (S2 amplitude band)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Tesseral geopotential in the Cowell propagator</span><code class="lg-mod">propagator <wbr>(Geopotential,<wbr> <wbr>EarthFixedFrame),<wbr> <wbr>gravity_<wbr>sh</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Opt-in EGM2008 tesseral field (to degree/order 70) as the central gravity of the numerical Cowell ForceModel, evaluated in an Earth-fixed frame rotated by the IAU 2000 Earth rotation angle (or the CIO GCRS-to-ITRS chain); default off, so no existing output moves</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with HolmesFeatherstoneAttractionModel over the same coefficients (tools/egm2008_to70.gfc) in a body frame rotated by the same Earth-rotation-angle formula, run as a separate program. Bars pre-registered (bdab936, amended before any comparison in 2c91443): |dr| &lt;= 0.5 m and |dv| &lt;= 5e-4 m/s, and both negative controls missing by &gt; 100 m; all met, bars then tightened to 0.1 m and 1e-4 m/s. Not validated: Earth-orientation fidelity (both sides use UT1 = TT without precession-nutation in the Era frame), the Cio frame option (internal check only), degrees above 70</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/cowell_tesseral_orekit_oracle.rs (6 cases x 25 hourly states over 24 h: EGM2008 8x8 and 70x70 on LEO 45 deg, polar 89.9 deg, sun-synchronous 98.2 deg, GTO, and 70x70 with Sun/Moon and SRP; worst |dr| 0.0355 m…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">High-degree spherical-harmonic gravity (EGM2008 to 2190)</span><code class="lg-mod">gravity_<wbr>sh,<wbr> <wbr>propagator <wbr>(FieldEvaluator)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Underflow-safe, pole-safe evaluation of a fully-normalized field to degree 2190 (SphericalHarmonicField::acceleration_hf / potential_hf, Holmes-Featherstone 2002 modified forward column with the gradient free of 1/cos phi), a streaming ICGEM .gfc loader (from_gfc_file / from_gfc_reader; the full EGM2008 is fetched at run time, not bundled), and the Holmes-Featherstone evaluator selectable on the opt-in Cowell Geopotential</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) HolmesFeatherstoneAttractionModel and pyshtools 4.14.1 (BSD-3-Clause) MakeGravGridPoint, run as separate programs; bars pre-registered in xval/high-degree-gravity/PREREGISTRATION.md (73b2c75d) and xval/cowell-tesseral-200-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (876be39d). Orekit agrees everywhere it is defined, but pyshtools misses its pre-registered bar at the two near-pole points (up to 7.3e-10 m/s^2; a 50-digit mpmath check puts the error in pyshtools), there is no oracle value exactly on the polar axis, and the 24 h orbit&#x27;s pre-registered degree-70 sensitivity control missed (0.726 m against &gt; 1 m). Each miss is recorded, not tuned away, so the row stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/gravity_sh_high_degree.rs (Orekit at 8 off-axis points: max |da| 2.2e-17 m/s^2 at N = 70, always run; 3.5e-17, 3.9e-17 and 7.3e-17 m/s^2 at N = 200, 360, 2190, data-gated on KSHANA_EGM2008_GFC)…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">DOP853 integrator</span><code class="lg-mod">integrator,<wbr> <wbr>propagator</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Hairer DOP853 8(5,3) explicit Runge-Kutta with the combined 5th/3rd-order error estimator, the SciPy/Hairer step controller, automatic initial step, a fail-closed step guard and 7th-order dense output; selectable as the propagator Integrator variant Dop853 / propagator::propagate_dop853</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>SciPy 1.18.1 (BSD-3-Clause) solve_ivp(method=DOP853) and its single-step, error-norm, dense-output and initial-step routines, an independent implementation; exact two-body states from mpmath 1.3.0 at 40 digits. Bars pre-registered in xval/dop853-scipy/PREREGISTRATION.md (8b23409) before the first comparison; all met (xval/dop853-scipy/RESULTS.md). SciPy&#x27;s first step is g