arlo seeds, the decoupled pack on identical sensors). MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/fusion_coupled_filters.rs (3-D filter over 60 seeds: second-half mean NEES 8.12 for 8 states, last-epoch χ² sum inside its 95 % band; a 0.1 ns clock fix cuts the 3-D position sigma from 5.58 m to 3.07 m; bias…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Loosely coupled GNSS/INS EKF with lever arm on the KF-GINS dataset</span><code class="lg-mod">fusion::<wbr>kfgins <wbr>(run,<wbr> <wbr>loaders,<wbr> <wbr>score),<wbr> <wbr>fusion::<wbr>closed_<wbr>loop <wbr>(propagate_<wbr>increments,<wbr> <wbr>fuse_<wbr>position_<wbr>lever_<wbr>arm),<wbr> <wbr>fusion::<wbr>gnss_<wbr>ins_<wbr>ekf <wbr>(update_<wbr>position_<wbr>lever_<wbr>arm)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The closed-loop 15-state error-state EKF driven by IMU increments with GNSS antenna positions fused through the lever-arm model H = [I 0 −[(C_b^n l_b)×] 0 0], configured from a KF-GINS YAML file (initial state and sigmas, ARW/VRW, Gauss-Markov biases)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The dataset&#x27;s published reference trajectory and the KF-GINS C++ program (GPL-3.0, commit 8291a93e, run as a separate tool); bars pre-registered in xval/kfgins/PREREGISTRATION.md, and KF-GINS was not run on this data before that registration (checked in its Results). All six bars held on the first run. DATA-GATED: the KF-GINS demo dataset (real Leador-A15 IMU and RTK GNSS with a reference trajectory) carries no licence, so it is not vendored; xval/kfgins/fetch.sh fetches it at test time with every file&#x27;s SHA-256 pinned, and the test fails on a mismatch; the reproduce path, which needs network access, is in xval/kfgins/PREREGISTRATION.md. Promoted to VALIDATED by the owner&#x27;s decision on data-gated evidence (2026-10-13)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/fusion_kfgins_dataset_oracle.rs (synthetic loaders and spinning-platform lever-arm tests always; data-gated on KSHANA_KFGINS_DIR, run 2026-10-13 on the KF-GINS demo dataset: horizontal / vertical RMS 0.0109 /…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Test-bench export read by a simulator&#x27;s motion-file readers (gps-sdr-sim)</span><code class="lg-mod">interop::<wbr>testbench <wbr>(write_<wbr>motion_<wbr>csv,<wbr> <wbr>write_<wbr>nmea,<wbr> <wbr>export)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The bench-export motion files given to an open-source GNSS simulator&#x27;s own readers come back as the exported trajectory: the NMEA GGA file (-g), the time/latitude/longitude/height cut of the motion CSV (-x) and its time/x/y/z cut (-u), on 6 gnss-ins scenarios (including the maritime, rail and aviation packs, read to the simulator&#x27;s 3000-sample cap) and 2 stationary ones. Motion only; no signal is written or generated.</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: gps-sdr-sim (MIT, commit 28ca29a6) readNmeaGGA, readUserMotion and readUserMotionLLH with its own WGS 84 llh2xyz, called through xval/testbench-gpssdrsim/driver.c (no signal function called), against the motion CSV&#x27;s Earth-fixed columns row by row, with bars pre-registered in xval/testbench-gpssdrsim/PREREGISTRATION.md before any run: every sample read on all 8 scenarios; NMEA worst 1.21 mm (bar 1 cm); LLH cut worst 0.145 mm (bar 1 mm); four-column Earth-fixed cut exact (bar 1 um, amendment 1); gnss-ins rows on the simulator&#x27;s 10 Hz grid to 2.3e-13 s. FINDING (run 1, pinned): the header-removed full-width CSV is not readable by -u, because the reader&#x27;s 100-character line buffer splits each 161-character row into two samples; docs/TEST-BENCH.md gives the four-column cut. WHAT IS NOT: the physics of the scenario&#x27;s truth trajectory; gps-sdr-sim&#x27;s signal output; any other simulator&#x27;s reader; any receiver.</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/testbench_gpssdrsim_oracle.rs (6 tests: oracle and scenarios, export bytes pinned, NMEA and LLH readers, four-column ECEF cut, run-1 FINDING on the full-width CSV, 10 Hz row grid)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS/INS trajectory library and CSV truth import</span><code class="lg-mod">fusion::<wbr>trajectory <wbr>(TrajectoryCfg,<wbr> <wbr>Trajectory,<wbr> <wbr>kinematic_<wbr>imu,<wbr> <wbr>parse_<wbr>truth_<wbr>csv),<wbr> <wbr>fusion::<wbr>pack <wbr>([trajectory])</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Selectable motion profiles for the gnss-ins pack (straight, figure-8, urban stop-and-go, aircraft climb and coordinated turn, survey drone), defined by Euler-rate and along-track-acceleration commands closed on the truth state with the kinematic IMU (centripetal, gravity and Coriolis terms), and imported CSV truth turned into the IMU that reproduces it; the historical drive unchanged when no profile is given</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Internal: rigid-body kinematics identities, the CSV round trip and the filter&#x27;s own covariance. MODELLED (no real drive log is compared)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>fusion::trajectory::tests (coordinated turn has no side force and load factor 1/cos φ; CSV import reproduces a flown profile to 1e-3 m/s and 1 m); tests/gnss_ins_trajectory_library.rs (every profile runs and fusion…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Strapdown INS mechanization</span><code class="lg-mod">inertial::<wbr>mechanization,<wbr> <wbr>inertial::<wbr>attitude,<wbr> <wbr>inertial::<wbr>imu_<wbr>errors</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Quaternion-attitude, WGS-84 NED strapdown inertial mechanization (coning/sculling-compensated) propagating a navigation state from (Δθ, Δv) increments</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NaveGo v1.4 (R. Gonzalez et al., LGPL-3) run under Octave — an independent published INS toolbox driven by the identical (Δθ,Δv) increment stream. Attitude matches bit-for-bit (same NED / scalar-first-quaternion / Earth-rate / transport-rate conventions); velocity/position agree to two documented differences — the deflection-of-vertical north-gravity term NaveGo includes and kshana omits by design (plumb-bob gravity; matched to the Groves closed form to every digit, with a sanity-floor assert) and O(dt²) integrator differences — not mechanization errors</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/classical_strapdown_ins_reference.rs (static/turn/coning profiles, 30 epochs each, vs NaveGo: attitude bit-identical 0 rad; velocity/position within named analytic bounds)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Gravity-field functional synthesis (gravity-aided / GNSS-free nav map)</span><code class="lg-mod">gravity_<wbr>sh</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Spherical-harmonic gravity magnitude + disturbance (mGal) from any ICGEM .gfc model; GRS80 normal gravity</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>GRS80 (Moritz 1980, IAG) Somigliana normal gravity + published γ_e/γ_p; real ICGEM EGM2008 field</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/icgem_gravity_reference.rs (GRS80 synthesis reproduces Somigliana to 3.5e-12; real ICGEM EGM2008 disturbance map physical)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Earth-Moon CR3BP 9:2 NRHO against the orbit CAPSTONE flew</span><code class="lg-mod">cr3bp <wbr>(differential_<wbr>correct_<wbr>halo,<wbr> <wbr>PeriodicOrbit)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The circular-restricted 9:2 L2 southern near-rectilinear halo orbit that cr3bp::differential_correct_halo corrects (the Gateway and CAPSTONE orbit family), compared for its mean geometry (period, perilune radius, apolune radius) with the orbit CAPSTONE actually flew. A measurement of how well the CR3BP member describes the real orbit; a CR3BP orbit is not an ephemeris-model orbit and is not compared epoch by epoch</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Measured against the JPL Horizons reconstructed CAPSTONE ephemeris (NAIF -1176, CAPSTONE_merged, Moon-centred ICRF, 2023-03-01..31 at 10 min), committed byte for byte (tests/fixtures/capstone_horizons/, NOTICE.md), pre-registered dab3215c before it was fetched, as a measurement with bars on the mean geometry. Not a validation: the CR3BP omits the Sun, the Moon&#x27;s eccentricity and the station keeping CAPSTONE performed, so the bars describe how representative the model orbit is, not an accuracy. Results: docs/design/evidence/cap29d-capstone-nrho/RESULTS.md</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/capstone_nrho_horizons.rs::cr3bp_nrho_matches_capstone_mean_geometry (CAPSTONE March 2023, 4 perilunes and 5 apolunes: period 6.5004 d vs 6.5699 d (1.07 %, bar 3 %), apolune 71 182 vs 71 215 km (0.05 %, bar 5 %)…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">CR3BP rotating states mapped to Moon-centred ICRF on the DE440 Earth-Moon line</span><code class="lg-mod">cr3bp <wbr>(to_<wbr>moon_<wbr>centred_<wbr>icrf,<wbr> <wbr>from_<wbr>moon_<wbr>centred_<wbr>icrf,<wbr> <wbr>to_<wbr>moon_<wbr>centred_<wbr>icrf_<wbr>de440)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>cr3bp::to_moon_centred_icrf maps a nondimensional Earth-Moon CR3BP rotating state to a Moon-centred ICRF position and velocity on the instantaneous Earth-Moon line (length unit the Earth-Moon distance, time unit the inverse orbital rate, the frame&#x27;s full angular velocity including its out-of-plane rate from the Moon&#x27;s acceleration), with the inverse from_moon_centred_icrf, and cr3bp::to_moon_centred_icrf_de440 reads the geocentric Moon state from a DE440 SPK. It maps states; it does not make a CR3BP orbit an ephemeris-model orbit</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) sxform of a two-vector dynamic frame (primary +X along the Moon&#x27;s position wrt the Earth, secondary +Y along its velocity, de440s), applied to the dimensional rotating state built from SPICE&#x27;s own Moon state, by a separate program that calls no Kshana code (tests/fixtures/cr3bp_to_mci_spice/, NOTICE.md). Pre-registered 3bfc9445, amended before any run in e8bfabe4 to include the frame&#x27;s out-of-plane angular velocity (without it the velocity would be up to 8e-2 m/s off). A Library check of the transform only. Results: docs/design/evidence/rm34-cr3bp-to-mci/RESULTS.md</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/cr3bp_to_mci_spice_oracle.rs::cr3bp_to_mci_matches_a_spice_two_vector_frame (72 cases: 12 epochs in January 2025 x the L1 and L2 points, two L2 southern halo and one DRO initial state of the JPL Three-Body…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Solar-system kind on a JPL kernel (planetary_kernel_path)</span><code class="lg-mod">solar_<wbr>system <wbr>(SolarSystemScenario::<wbr>planetary_<wbr>kernel_<wbr>path,<wbr> <wbr>KernelOut); <wbr>ephem_<wbr>provider <wbr>(KernelEphemeris)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>solar-system</code> kind with planetary_kernel_path: each reported body whose own NAIF code the SPK holds takes its heliocentric J2000 position and velocity from the kernel through the engine&#x27;s own reader (label KERNEL), a link whose two ends are both in the kernel is computed on it, barycentres are never substituted for a planet, and every other body stays analytic with its own label; the report gains a kernel block (path, SHA-256, the bodies and links taken from it) only when the option is set. With de440s.bsp that is Mercury, Venus, the Earth and the Moon</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) spkezr heliocentric states on the full de440s.bsp, a separate program that calls no Kshana code (tests/fixtures/solar_system_kernel/, NOTICE.md); the engine reads bit-for-bit de440s cuts. Pre-registered f4a6297a. A Library check of the wiring: DE440 itself and the analytic bodies are not validated here. Results: docs/design/evidence/cap31-solar-system-kernel/RESULTS.md</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/solar_system_kernel_oracle.rs::kernel_backed_solar_system_matches_spice (Mercury, Venus, the Earth and the Moon at 2026-01-01, 2030-06-15 and 2040-12-31: worst 3.3e-5 m and 1.0e-11 m/s against 1 mm and 1e-6 m/s…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Porkchop on the DE440 ephemeris</span><code class="lg-mod">maneuver <wbr>(porkchop_<wbr>kernel,<wbr> <wbr>PorkchopGrid); <wbr>ephem_<wbr>provider <wbr>(KernelEphemeris)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>maneuver::porkchop_kernel: the launch-date x arrival-date sweep of departure C3 (km²/s²) and arrival V-infinity (km/s) with the single-revolution prograde Lambert solver and the 0.5 deg collinearity guard, on the heliocentric J2000 states of any two NAIF bodies read from a JPL DE440 SPK by the crate&#x27;s own reader. One revolution, prograde, no launch-vehicle or arrival constraints: a sweep, not a mission design</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) heliocentric states on de440s.bsp and pykep 3.0.1 lambert_problem (ESA, the C++ kep3 core, MPL-2.0), an independent Lambert solver, run as a separate program that calls no Kshana code (tests/fixtures/porkchop_de440/, NOTICE.md); the engine reads a bit-for-bit de440s cut. Pre-registered b9317cee. A Library check of the sweep. Results: docs/design/evidence/cap20b-porkchop-de440/RESULTS.md</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/porkchop_de440_oracle.rs::de440_porkchop_matches_spice_and_pykep (Earth to the Mars barycentre, 16 departures 2026-09-01..2027-01-29 x 19 arrivals 2027-06-01..2028-02-26: 304 cells finite on both sides, worst C3…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Impulsive transfer optimiser on the DE440 ephemeris</span><code class="lg-mod">maneuver <wbr>(optimise_<wbr>transfer_<wbr>kernel,<wbr> <wbr>TransferObjective,<wbr> <wbr>TransferType,<wbr> <wbr>TransferOptimum); <wbr>ephem_<wbr>provider <wbr>(KernelEphemeris)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>maneuver::optimise_transfer_kernel: the departure and arrival dates of a single-revolution ballistic two-impulse transfer that minimise departure C3 or arrival V-infinity within Type I or Type II, as a continuous optimum (a 2-day grid over both windows, then 2-D Nelder-Mead), on heliocentric states from the DE440 SPK. A date optimiser for one leg, not a mission design: no launch-vehicle, parking-orbit, deep-space-manoeuvre or multi-revolution options</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>An independent optimiser: NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) heliocentric states on the same de440s cut, pykep 3.0.1 lambert_problem (ESA, MPL-2.0) and SciPy 1.18.1 Nelder-Mead, calling no Kshana code. The NASA Earth-to-Mars handbook (MIDAS, ephemeris unstated, 2-day date grid) is a secondary published reference and does not set the status</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/transfer_optimiser_oracle.rs::transfer_optimiser_matches_spice_pykep_scipy (Earth to the Mars barycentre, departures 2026-09-15..2027-01-15 x arrivals 2027-05-01..2028-03-01, Type I/II x min C3 / min V-infinity…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Multi-revolution Lambert transfer solver</span><code class="lg-mod">maneuver <wbr>(lambert_<wbr>multirev,<wbr> <wbr>LambertSolution,<wbr> <wbr>LambertBranch)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Every solution of Lambert&#x27;s problem with up to three complete revolutions (maneuver::lambert_multirev: the single-revolution solution, then the left and right branches of each revolution count, Izzo 2015), with the boundary velocities v1 and v2, for prograde and retrograde transfers, in canonical and heliocentric SI units, including transfers within 1 % of a revolution count&#x27;s minimum time of flight and near-180 deg geometries. A two-body solver only (no perturbations)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pykep 3.0.1 lambert_problem (ESA, the C++ kep3 core, MPL-2.0), an independent library, run as a separate program by scripts/gen_lambert_multirev_ref.py; only its numerical output is committed. Pre-registered 9879ae57 (case set, bars, outcome rule) before any comparison; the reference was regenerated independently and is byte-identical. Results: docs/design/evidence/lambert-multirev/RESULTS.md</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/lambert_multirev_oracle.rs::multirev_lambert_matches_pykep_on_the_preregistered_cases (382 cases: 300 random 3-D, 18 near 180 deg, 24 near the minimum time of M = 1..3, 40 heliocentric; 1058 solutions; counts and…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Lambert two-body transfer solver</span><code class="lg-mod">maneuver <wbr>(lambert)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Izzo-2015 single-revolution Lambert solver (r1, r2, time-of-flight → boundary velocities) across LEO→GEO→heliocentric transfers, prograde and retrograde</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>lamberthub 1.0.0 izzo2015 (J. Martínez Garrido, MIT) — an independent third-party Lambert solver. The single-revolution (M=0) Lambert problem has a unique solution, so library-vs-library agreement is a genuine external check; matched to &lt;1e-4 m/s (observed ~1e-11), the same kind of validation DOP gets vs gnss_lib_py</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/lambert_reference.rs (13 transfers vs lamberthub izzo2015; worst |Δv| 7e-12 m/s)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Reference frames &amp; timescales</span><code class="lg-mod">frames,<wbr> <wbr>precession,<wbr> <wbr>nutation,<wbr> <wbr>cio,<wbr> <wbr>timescales</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>IAU 2006/2000A precession-nutation, CIO GCRS↔ITRS, leap-second timescales</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>IAU SOFA / ERFA reference vectors; Vallado AIAA 2006-6753 (0.1–4 mm)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/frame_reference_vectors.rs (Vallado end-to-end; SOFA/ERFA vectors)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Two-part Julian dates and leap seconds on the Earth-orbit time path</span><code class="lg-mod">jd2 <wbr>(Jd2::<wbr>from_<wbr>utc_<wbr>calendar,<wbr> <wbr>utc_<wbr>to_<wbr>tai,<wbr> <wbr>tai_<wbr>to_<wbr>utc,<wbr> <wbr>utc_<wbr>to_<wbr>ut1,<wbr> <wbr>tai_<wbr>to_<wbr>tt,<wbr> <wbr>earth_<wbr>rotation_<wbr>angle); <wbr>sgp4 <wbr>(Sgp4::<wbr>new_<wbr>at,<wbr> <wbr>Sgp4::<wbr>propagate_<wbr>at)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>A UTC calendar date and time to a two-part UTC Julian date with the SOFA dtf2d convention for days that end in a leap second (86 401 s long, 23:59:60.x valid), UTC to TAI and back, and UT1 from UTC through TAI, on two-part dates from 1972-01-01: the time path SGP4 (Sgp4::propagate_at, measured from a two-part epoch) and the IAU 2006/2000A Earth-fixed rotation (sgp4::teme_to_itrs_matrix_eop with the two-part Earth rotation angle) take</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: erfa.dtf2d(&quot;UTC&quot;), erfa.utctai and erfa.taiutc on the committed instants, the gap formed part by part on the two-part dates, bar 1e-6 s fixed before the run (pre-registered 896f3c2e). Worst gap 9.6e-12 s (dtf2d) and 1.9e-11 s (utctai, taiutc). Every UTC day taken as 86 400 s, or the leap-day fraction left unscaled, turns the test red. UT1 through TAI (iauUtcut1) is checked by the Earth-orbit path row&#x27;s ERFA leg. Scope: UTC from 1972-01-01; the earlier drifting-rate UTC is not modelled</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/jd2_sofa_time_oracle.rs (224 UTC instants: six around each of the 27 leap seconds 1972-2016, two fixed, 60 seeded across 1972-2026; dtf2d, utctai and taiutc each within 1e-6 s, worst 1.9e-11 s); jd2::tests…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Two-part Julian dates through Earth rotation on the orbit path</span><code class="lg-mod">jd2 <wbr>(gmst82,<wbr> <wbr>From&lt;TwoPartJd&gt;,<wbr> <wbr>From&lt;Jd2&gt;); <wbr>cio <wbr>(gcrs_<wbr>to_<wbr>itrs_<wbr>matrix_<wbr>jd2); <wbr>frames <wbr>(teme_<wbr>to_<wbr>itrf_<wbr>jd2,<wbr> <wbr>itrf_<wbr>to_<wbr>teme_<wbr>jd2); <wbr>orbit <wbr>(Propagator::<wbr>position_<wbr>in_<wbr>frame_<wbr>jd2,<wbr> <wbr>own_<wbr>jd2_<wbr>utc); <wbr>ephemeris <wbr>(EphemerisScenario::<wbr>two_<wbr>part_<wbr>jd)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Earth rotation from a two-part UT1 Julian date on the orbit path, so the Earth-fixed position keeps the microsecond a single-f64 date loses (about 3e-9 rad near 2020): jd2::gmst82 (IAU 1982 GMST, the TEME convention, on the two parts), cio::gcrs_to_itrs_matrix_jd2 (the CIO GCRS to ITRS matrix with the Earth rotation angle from the two parts), and, built on them and checked internally, frames::teme_to_itrf_jd2 / itrf_to_teme_jd2, orbit::Propagator::position_in_frame_jd2 and own_jd2_utc, the ephemeris scenario&#x27;s opt-in two_part_jd (the ECEF position, ground track and station view; default off, unchanged), and lossless From conversions between jd2::Jd2 and timescales::TwoPartJd. Precession-nutation and the Cowell force-model epoch stay single-f64, where a 40 us rounding is below 1e-15 rad or far below the analytic Sun/Moon ephemeris error</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: gmst82 and c2t06a on the committed two-part dates, polar motion 0.1 and 0.3 arcsec. Pre-registered 765860c3 (dates, bars) before the functions were written and before the oracle was run. The internal tests (the scenario option, the TEME/ITRF round trip, the type conversions) are supporting self-consistency</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/jd2_earth_rotation_erfa_oracle.rs::two_part_earth_rotation_agrees_with_erfa (40 two-part UT1/TT dates 1980-2050 including +1, +10, +50 us steps at two dates: gmst82 within 1e-12 rad, worst 0; GCRS to ITRS…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Frame matrices against ERFA vectors: IAU 2006 bias-precession, TEME to GCRS, and the CIO chain with celestial-pole offsets</span><code class="lg-mod">precession <wbr>(precession_<wbr>matrix); <wbr>nutation <wbr>(teme_<wbr>to_<wbr>gcrs_<wbr>matrix); <wbr>cio <wbr>(gcrs_<wbr>to_<wbr>cirs_<wbr>matrix_<wbr>with_<wbr>offsets)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Three frame matrices compared element by element or by rotation angle with ERFA at arbitrary epochs: precession::precession_matrix (IAU 2006 bias-precession by Fukushima-Williams angles), nutation::teme_to_gcrs_matrix (the TEME to GCRS rotation P^T N^T Rz(-EE) with IAU 2006 precession, IAU 2000B nutation and the equation of the equinoxes with its two leading complementary terms), and cio::gcrs_to_cirs_matrix_with_offsets (the GCRS to CIRS rotation with the IERS celestial-pole offsets dX, dY added to the model CIP X, Y before the rotation, the SOFA cookbook convention; new). The model choices themselves (2000B nutation on the TEME path, the two-term complementary series) are not validated, only their implementation</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: pmat06; numat(obl06, nut00b) with the equation of the equinoxes from eect00 (the full complementary series), composed as P^T N^T Rz(-EE); xys06a then c2ixys(X + dX, Y + dY, s). Pre-registered bbaeb5b5 (epochs, offsets and bars) before the oracle was run. The 1.1e-10 rad TEME residual is the predicted effect of keeping two complementary terms (about 3 mm at GNSS orbit)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/frames_erfa_vector_oracle.rs::frame_matrices_agree_with_erfa_vectors (40 TT epochs 1950-2100, dX and dY up to 0.5 mas: precession elements within 1e-13, worst 0; TEME to GCRS within 5e-10 rad, worst 1.10e-10 rad…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Pre-1972 UTC (drifting-rate era) on the two-part Julian-date time path</span><code class="lg-mod">timescales <wbr>(tai_<wbr>minus_<wbr>utc_<wbr>on_<wbr>date); <wbr>jd2 <wbr>(Jd2::<wbr>from_<wbr>utc_<wbr>calendar,<wbr> <wbr>utc_<wbr>to_<wbr>tai,<wbr> <wbr>tai_<wbr>to_<wbr>utc)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>UTC from 1960-01-01 to 1971-12-31, when UTC ran at an offset rate from TAI and stepped by fractions of a second, on the two-part Julian-date path: TAI - UTC from the drift table of each period (timescales::tai_minus_utc_on_date), the UTC calendar date to a two-part UTC Julian date with the day length 86 400 s + dleap (Jd2::from_utc_calendar), UTC to TAI with the day fraction scaled from UTC to SI seconds (jd2::utc_to_tai) and TAI back to UTC (jd2::tai_to_utc), following SOFA dtf2d, utctai and taiutc. Before 1960 TAI - UTC is taken as 0 (SOFA&#x27;s value; UTC undefined) and is outside the claim. The single-f64 timescales::tai_minus_utc still clamps to 10 s before 1972 and is not covered</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: erfa.dtf2d(&quot;UTC&quot;), erfa.utctai and erfa.taiutc on the committed instants, the gap formed part by part. Pre-registered 247a01a6 (instants, tolerance and the 1972+ checksum) before the engine change and before the oracle was run. The drift table is transcribed from the same SOFA iauDat table the oracle uses, so the comparison covers the conversion arithmetic (day length, UTC-to-SI scaling, iteration), not the historical table itself</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/jd2_pre1972_sofa_oracle.rs::pre_1972_utc_conversions_agree_with_erfa (117 UTC instants: four around each of the 15 starts of a TAI-UTC expression 1960-1972 and 60 seeded across 1960-1971; dtf2d, utctai and taiutc…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">GLONASS broadcast satellite clock and broadcast-orbit propagation</span><code class="lg-mod">glonass <wbr>(GlonassEphemeris::<wbr>sv_<wbr>clock_<wbr>bias_<wbr>s,<wbr> <wbr>position_<wbr>ecef,<wbr> <wbr>parse_<wbr>glonass_<wbr>nav)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The GLONASS broadcast satellite clock bias -tau_n + gamma_n (t - t_b) at a time read on the satellite clock (glonass::GlonassEphemeris::sv_clock_bias_s, the offset corrected for the clock bias by two fixed-point iterations), and the PZ-90 Earth-fixed position of the broadcast state vector propagated by RK4 through the GLONASS ICD equations of motion within +/-15 min of the reference epoch (GlonassEphemeris::position_ecef), from real RINEX 3 GLONASS navigation records. No PZ-90.11 to ITRF transformation is applied (they agree at the centimetre level since 2014; RTKLIB applies none either). Not wired into a scenario output: library function</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 (T. Takasu, BSD-2-Clause, commit 71db0ffa) geph2clk and geph2pos, compiled from C source in a separate harness (xval/rtklib-glonass) that reads the same RINEX file with RTKLIB&#x27;s own reader, so the parse, the sign of -tau_n, the units and the iteration are compared as well as the clock and the orbit. Pre-registered 5cad23b7 before the function existed and before the harness was built. Both sides evaluate the same clock closed form, so the clock comparison covers the implementation, not the formula. The orbit integrators differ (RTKLIB steps 60 s then the remainder, Kshana equal steps of at most 60 s) and so do the constants (RTKLIB mu 3.9860044e14, Kshana the PZ-90.11 3.986004418e14); the 1.1 mm worst case is that difference, as predicted before the run. Scope: the broadcast ephemeris within its validity interval, not the true orbit or clock (no comparison with precise products)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/glonass_broadcast_rtklib_oracle.rs::glonass_broadcast_clock_and_orbit_match_rtklib (292 cases: the 4 GLONASS records of the real 2013-01-01 IGS MGEX broadcast file x 73 offsets within +/-900 s; clock within 1e-14…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">One Earth-orbit path: SGP4 element sets to the GCRS and ITRS on the IAU 2006/2000A chain with Earth orientation parameters</span><code class="lg-mod">sgp4 <wbr>(MeanElementSet,<wbr> <wbr>SgpOrbit,<wbr> <wbr>Eop,<wbr> <wbr>teme_<wbr>to_<wbr>itrs_<wbr>matrix_<wbr>eop,<wbr> <wbr>gcrs_<wbr>to_<wbr>itrs_<wbr>matrix_<wbr>eop); <wbr>jd2 <wbr>(utc_<wbr>to_<wbr>ut1,<wbr> <wbr>earth_<wbr>rotation_<wbr>angle); <wbr>nutation <wbr>(teme_<wbr>to_<wbr>gcrs_<wbr>matrix); <wbr>cio <wbr>(gcrs_<wbr>to_<wbr>cirs_<wbr>matrix); <wbr>frames <wbr>(polar_<wbr>motion_<wbr>matrix)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The propagation and frame path every routed Earth-orbit kind shares: an SGP4 mean element set with a two-part UTC epoch (sgp4::MeanElementSet, read from constellation-design elements by MeanElementSet::from_earth_fixed) propagated by SGP4/SDP4 to TEME (sgp4::SgpOrbit), and the TEME -&gt; ITRS and GCRS -&gt; ITRS rotations of the IAU 2006/2000A chain with UT1 - UTC and polar motion (sgp4::teme_to_itrs_matrix_eop, gcrs_to_itrs_matrix_eop, the zero-parameter forms teme_to_itrs_matrix and gcrs_to_itrs_matrix). TEME is the true equator of date with the mean equinox, reached from the GCRS through the IAU 2006 bias-precession, the IAU 2000B nutation and the equation of the equinoxes</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Leg 1: python-sgp4 2.24 (MIT licence; D. Vallado&#x27;s reference C++ SGP4, the code that produced the AIAA 2006-6753 verification vectors), WGS-72, improved mode. Leg 2: pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (ERFA, BSD-3-Clause, the SOFA routines): c2t06a with TT from utctai/taitt and UT1 from utcut1, and for TEME c2t06a . pnm06a^T . rz(-ee06a). Bars fixed before the run (pre-registered d92aef9b). Disclosed: the first run failed leg 2 at 2016-12-31T23:59:59.5 by one second of rotation (UT1 taken from the UTC quasi Julian date on a leap-second day); the engine was corrected (jd2::utc_to_ut1) and the second run agrees at the unchanged bars. Polar motion dropped (46 failures) or WGS-84 constants in place of WGS-72 (4432 failures) turn the test red. This three-leg design replaced an end-to-end Orekit comparison that failed on Orekit&#x27;s SDP4 at e = 0 and on its IERS 1996 TEME (the finding stays pinned in tests/leo_polar_coverage_full_claim_orekit_oracle.rs)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/earth_orbit_path_sgp4_erfa_oracle.rs (leg 1: 4432 TEME positions of the 232 committed element sets against python-sgp4 2.24, worst 6.1e-8 m, bar 1 cm; leg 2: 108 instants, 23 of them with Earth orientation…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="signal"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Ranging-code design trade</span><code class="lg-mod">navsignal <wbr>(CodeFamily)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>m-sequence/Gold sidelobe &amp; cross-correlation bounds, length↔ambiguity</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Published GPS C/A Gold cross-correlation (−23.9 dB); Gold 1967 bound</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>navsignal::code_tests (GPS C/A Gold ≈ −23.9 dB; φ(1023)/10 = 60)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS geometry / dilution of precision (DOP)</span><code class="lg-mod">orbit <wbr>(dop)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>GDOP/PDOP/HDOP/VDOP/TDOP from line-of-sight geometry via Q=(HᵀH)⁻¹ with a local ENU split</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 (Stanford NAV Lab) DOP — independent library, matched to 1e-6 relative</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/dop_reference.rs (8 geometries, well-conditioned → near-singular)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Position error from geometry: RMS error = sigma_UERE x DOP for the engine&#x27;s least-squares fix</span><code class="lg-mod">dop_<wbr>error_<wbr>mc <wbr>(run_<wbr>geometry,<wbr> <wbr>check,<wbr> <wbr>TOLERANCES); <wbr>dop_<wbr>error_<wbr>ci <wbr>(run,<wbr> <wbr>GEOMETRIES); <wbr>pvt <wbr>(solve_<wbr>spp); <wbr>orbit <wbr>(dop,<wbr> <wbr>dop_<wbr>cofactor)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>dop_error_mc::run_geometry and check: a seeded Monte Carlo (N = 20,000 epochs per geometry, sigma = 1.5 m, ChaCha8 seed 36 + i) of the engine&#x27;s own iterated least-squares fix (pvt::solve_spp, unit weights, solved from the true position) on the 8 geometries of tests/fixtures/dop/dop_reference.csv, comparing the east, north, up and clock error variances, the H/V/P RMS, the east-north correlation, the mean errors and the fraction inside the 95 % horizontal ellipse with sigma^2 Q, where Q is the covariance factor of the orbit::dop kernel (orbit::dop_cofactor, the same arithmetic) on the same geometry</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Internal consistency against a closed form: under the linear Gaussian model the least-squares covariance is sigma^2 (H^T H)^-1 (Kaplan and Hegarty (eds.), Understanding GPS: Principles and Applications, 2nd ed., Artech House, 2006, ch. 7). Method and tolerances of the 0.36 geometry design note, section 5.2, fixed in a pre-registration commit before the first run: each variance within 5 % (5 standard errors of a sample variance at N = 20,000), each RMS within 2.5 %, the east-north correlation within 0.035 (5/sqrt(N)), each mean within 5 standard errors, the 95 % ellipse fraction within 0.95 +/- 0.0077 (5 binomial standard errors); negative controls (PDOP doubled; errors growing as 1/sin(elevation) against the unweighted Q) must fail. HOW IT COMPOSES: Q is the covariance factor of the orbit::dop kernel, VALIDATED against gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 on these same 8 geometries in tests/dop_reference.rs (row &#x27;GNSS geometry / dilution of precision&#x27;); this row adds the step from those factors to metres through the engine&#x27;s solver and does not borrow that row&#x27;s status. WHY MODELLED: no external dataset is involved; agreement shows the estimator and noise realise sigma x DOP under the textbook model, not that any receiver&#x27;s error equals UERE x DOP, and sigma_UERE is an input</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/uere_dop_monte_carlo.rs (position_error_is_sigma_times_dop_on_the_gnss_lib_py_geometries: all 104 checks pass on the first run after pre-registration f97bf4c1, worst variance 3.2 %, RMS 1.6 %, correlation 0.012…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Geometry-aware trust classification: satellite geometry beside the receiver-trust score and a class on every deduction</span><code class="lg-mod">receiver_<wbr>trust <wbr>(geometry: <wbr>GeometryTracker,<wbr> <wbr>geometry_<wbr>ratio,<wbr> <wbr>sky_<wbr>ratio,<wbr> <wbr>geometry_<wbr>points,<wbr> <wbr>GeometryLabel; <wbr>score: <wbr>DeductionClass,<wbr> <wbr>score_<wbr>with; <wbr>monitors; <wbr>live); <wbr>telemetry <wbr>(sample,<wbr> <wbr>prometheus,<wbr> <wbr>syslog,<wbr> <wbr>otlp)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>receiver_trust::geometry: per-epoch satellite geometry (good, degraded with reasons few-satellites/high-pdop/high-hdop/sky-blocked, or unknown when the log gives no GSV, NAV-SAT or GSA) derived from the reported elevations and azimuths through orbit::dop or taken from GSA, with a label always reported and never scored (good, fair, poor, unknown) and the HDOP, PDOP, VDOP, satellite count and signal loss in words; a vessel-score geometry monitor that deducts nothing unless the geometry is poor (ratio max(hdop/4, pdop/6, 5/n_used) above 1), then ramps to a cap of 15 points at 1.5 that geometry alone cannot take below the degraded band (cap, ramp end and floor in [geometry]; no alarm); a class on every deduction (geometry, environment, inconsistency) with loss-of-lock to satellites below 15 deg classed geometry, satellites in view but not tracked classed environment, and a poor-geometry deduction whose stated HDOP is beyond max(1.0, 25 % of the derived HDOP) from the stated sky classed inconsistency</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Internal consistency: the classification is checked against the rules it states and against hand-derived ratios on synthetic edits of the committed demo log, and the no-change pins hold the 0.35.0 outputs: no score changes unless the geometry is poor; the label is always reported. WHY MODELLED: the thresholds are chosen, not fitted (provenance in each run&#x27;s geometry_model; the IEC 61108-1 and GPS SPS figures as cited, to be checked against the standards&#x27; text), and there is no external labelled dataset of natural geometry degradation against attacks to validate the classification against. The derived DOP reuses the orbit::dop kernel, VALIDATED against gnss_lib_py, whose status this row does not borrow. A class of geometry is a statement about the sky, not a finding that no attack occurred</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/natural_degradation_trust.rs (a_stated_skyline_reads_the_fjord_as_geometry: on the synthetic fjord passage of scenarios/training/fjord-natural-degradation.toml the same 624 epochs are deducted with and without…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="signal"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Broadcast ionosphere model (Klobuchar, IS-GPS-200)</span><code class="lg-mod">gnss_<wbr>sim <wbr>(klobuchar_<wbr>delay_<wbr>m)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Klobuchar single-frequency L1 slant ionospheric group delay from the eight broadcast α/β coefficients</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>RTKLIB ionmodel (tomojitakasu/RTKLIB, src/rtkcmn.c) — independent reference implementation compiled from source; matched to &lt; 1e-4 m</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/klobuchar_reference.rs (10 cases across elevation/azimuth/local-time, two coefficient sets)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="integrity"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">RAIM/ARAIM integrity statistical kernel (χ² / non-central χ² / normal laws)</span><code class="lg-mod">raim <wbr>(chi2_<wbr>cdf,<wbr> <wbr>chi2_<wbr>quantile,<wbr> <wbr>noncentral_<wbr>chi2_<wbr>cdf,<wbr> <wbr>normal_<wbr>cdf,<wbr> <wbr>normal_<wbr>quantile,<wbr> <wbr>pbias)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The distributional core every protection level rests on: the snapshot fault-detection threshold χ²₁₋ₚfₐ(dof), the missed-detection non-centrality pbias=√λ, and the K_fa/K_md/K_V solution-separation multipliers</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>SciPy 1.17.0 (scipy.stats.chi2/.norm/.ncx2 + optimize.brentq) — independent library (Cephes/Boost), a different algorithm from Kshana&#x27;s incomplete-gamma series; matched to ≤1e-6 rel. Kernel only. The ARAIM MHSS P_HMI budget *allocation* is no longer without a published numeric oracle — the WG-C ARAIM Technical Subgroup&#x27;s own worked example now backs the separate &#x27;ARAIM MHSS protection levels against published reference vectors&#x27; row, matched at the reference&#x27;s own TOL_PL = 5e-2 m — so this row&#x27;s scope is the statistical kernel and that row carries the allocation (see docs/ARAIM_REFERENCE.md)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/raim_reference.rs (171 cases: χ² CDF/quantile, normal CDF/quantile, non-central χ² CDF, pbias across the P_fa/P_md/redundancy ranges)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="integrity"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">SBAS protection level (DO-229E weighted-LS HPL/VPL)</span><code class="lg-mod">sbas <wbr>(sbas_<wbr>protection_<wbr>level)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>DO-229E Appendix J weighted-least-squares protection levels: D=(GᵀWG)⁻¹ from per-satellite elevation/azimuth and error budget, horizontal error-ellipse major axis and vertical σ, scaled by the published K-factors</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>RTKLIB SBAS-PL fork — zsiki/rtklib_ws waasprotlevels() (Siki &amp; Takács 2017, &quot;DO-229D Appendix J&quot;), run by rnx2rtkp -ws on real EGNOS GEO-PRN120 messages + real BUTE/Budapest RINEX; independent third-party implementation, HPL matched to &lt; 2e-3 m. gLAB v6.0.0 (core/filter.c) confirmed identical convention. Both oracles round K_V→5.33 vs Kshana&#x27;s exact Φ⁻¹(1−5e-8)=5.3267 (~0.06%), so the vertical is checked as the K-factor-free d_U</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/sbas_reference.rs (6 real-EGNOS epochs: HPL matched directly, vertical d_U checked K-factor-free)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">ML detector-evaluation metrics (ROC/AUC/confusion/Pfa-Pmd)</span><code class="lg-mod">impairment_<wbr>eval <wbr>(auc,<wbr> <wbr>confusion_<wbr>at,<wbr> <wbr>roc_<wbr>curve)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>AUC (Mann-Whitney, ties ½), confusion matrix at threshold, P_d/P_md/P_fa/precision/accuracy/F1</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scikit-learn 1.9.0 (Pedregosa et al., JMLR 2011) — independent library, exact match</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/eval_metrics_reference.rs (5 datasets, 24 thresholds; exact counts + &lt;1e-9)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Anomaly-detection scoring on real spacecraft telemetry</span><code class="lg-mod">impairment_<wbr>eval,<wbr> <wbr>eval_<wbr>stats</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>ROC AUC + bootstrap CI separating real labelled anomalies; transparent detector (reproduces-labels)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scikit-learn roc_auc_score on the OPSSAT-AD test split (Ruszczak et al. 2025, CC BY 4.0) — real OPS-SAT telemetry</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/opssat_ad_reference.rs (real ESA OPS-SAT, AUC reproduces scikit-learn to 1e-9); tests/ai_ml_rf_impairment_detection_evaluation_reference.rs (122 cases on the real OPSSAT-AD test split: full operating-point…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Quantum-trade numerical kernels (NNLS / χ² bands / van-Loan Q)</span><code class="lg-mod">quantum_<wbr>trade <wbr>(qparams_<wbr>from_<wbr>adev_<wbr>curve),<wbr> <wbr>detection <wbr>(chi2_<wbr>inv_<wbr>cdf),<wbr> <wbr>clock_<wbr>state <wbr>(ClockState3)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Measured-ADEV NNLS fit, NEES/NIS χ² consistency bands, and the clock van-Loan discrete process-noise (holdover-coast) covariance — the trade engine&#x27;s computational spine</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scipy 1.17.1 — optimize.nnls / stats.chi2.ppf / linalg.expm; NNLS+Q exact, χ² &lt;5e-4 at operating dof. Kernels only — device-performance numbers stay Modelled (next row)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/scipy_reference.rs (NNLS; χ² at operating dof ≥ 48; van-Loan Q)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Geomagnetic reference field (IGRF-14 synthesis)</span><code class="lg-mod">igrf,<wbr> <wbr>igrf_<wbr>data</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Spherical-harmonic synthesis of the IGRF-14 main field — X/Y/Z/F components, declination and inclination — feeding the magnetic-anomaly alt-PNT layer</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>ppigrf 2.1.0 (K. M. Laundal, MIT) — the IAGA-VMOD pure-Python IGRF reference implementation shipping the official IGRF14.shc coefficients (IAGA 14th generation, Zenodo 10.5281/zenodo.14012302); an independent third-party codebase computing the uniquely-defined IGRF-14 field, matched over a global grid</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/alternative_complementary_pnt_reference.rs (2520 global points × altitudes vs ppigrf @ epoch 2025.0; worst |ΔXYZF| 3.9e-4 nT, |ΔD| 2.8e-6°, |ΔI| 3.6e-7°)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Detection statistics — Gaussian AUC &amp; minimum detectable fault</span><code class="lg-mod">quantum_<wbr>faults,<wbr> <wbr>eval_<wbr>stats,<wbr> <wbr>detection</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Analytic binormal ROC AUC = Φ(μ/(σ√2)) and the minimum detectable fault σ·(Φ⁻¹(1−P_fa)+Φ⁻¹(P_d)) underpinning the quantum-fault and anomaly detectors</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scipy 1.17 (Cephes ndtr/ndtri) + scikit-learn roc_auc_score (Pedregosa et al., JMLR 2011), both BSD-3-Clause — independent libraries computing the same uniquely-defined Gaussian-tail / AUC quantities, matched to the A&amp;S-erf floor (~7e-8). The quantum-vs-classical advantage built on top stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/quantum_faults_reference.rs (109 cases vs scipy 1.17 norm.cdf/ppf + scikit-learn roc_auc_score; worst |Δ| AUC 6.9e-8, min-detectable-fault 1.3e-8, empirical-AUC 1.1e-16)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Rank-order statistics kernel (Kendall-τ / Dirichlet / percentile)</span><code class="lg-mod">resilience::<wbr>stats</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Rank-correlation and resampling kernels under the resilience decision-instability study: Kendall τ-b, Dirichlet mean, competition ranking and percentile confidence intervals</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scipy 1.18 (stats.kendalltau variant=&#x27;b&#x27;, rankdata) + numpy.percentile (BSD-3-Clause) — independent implementations (merge-sort τ) of the uniquely-defined rank statistics, matched to 1e-12. The decision-instability study built on these kernels stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/resilience_score_decision_instability_reference.rs (124 cases vs scipy 1.18 / numpy 2.4: 61 Kendall τ-b to 2e-16, ranking exact, Dirichlet mean + percentile-CI to 1e-12)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA priority-derivation kernel (AHP eigenvector / consistency ratio)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>ahp</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Analytic Hierarchy Process priority weights = normalised principal (Perron) eigenvector of a reciprocal pairwise-comparison matrix by power iteration, with the Saaty Consistency Index / Consistency Ratio and the CR&lt;0.10 acceptance gate</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Saaty (1980) canonical Random Index table (RI(5)=1.12) reproduced exactly + SciPy/LAPACK scipy.linalg.eig (BSD-3-Clause) as an independent eigensolver computing the same uniquely-defined Perron eigenvector/eigenvalue, matched to &lt;1e-9. The Pareto / sensitivity / MAUT decision layer built on this kernel stays MODELLED (the WSM/WPM/TOPSIS/VIKOR/PROMETHEE/ELECTRE aggregators are separately externally validated — see the rows below)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_ahp_reference.rs (Saaty 1980 Random Index table n=1..10 EXACT; priority vector + λ_max + CR vs SciPy/LAPACK eig on a consistent 3×3 and inconsistent 3×3 / 4×4, matched to &lt;1e-9)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA weighted-aggregation kernels (WSM / WPM)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>wsm,<wbr> <wbr>mcda::<wbr>wpm</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Weighted Sum Model (min–max-normalised additive aggregate + ranking) and Weighted Product Model (sum-normalised, reciprocal-for-cost multiplicative aggregate) — the two value-aggregation trade-study scorers</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pymcdm (methods.WSM + normalizations.minmax_normalization; methods.WPM + normalizations.sum_normalization) — an independent, widely-used third-party Python MCDA library computing the same uniquely-defined weighted aggregates; matched to &lt;1e-9. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mcda_wsm/ and tests/fixtures/mcda_wpm/. Garbage-in-garbage-out on the inputs; the sensitivity/robustness layer stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_wsm_reference.rs (WSM scores + ranking) and tests/mcda_wpm_reference.rs (WPM scores + ranking), each vs pymcdm to &lt;1e-9 on a fixed 4×3 benefit/cost decision matrix</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA distance-to-ideal ranking (TOPSIS)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>topsis</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution — min–max normalisation, weighted positive/negative ideal solutions, relative closeness Cᵢ = d⁻/(d⁺+d⁻) and ranking</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pymcdm methods.TOPSIS + normalizations.minmax_normalization — an independent third-party MCDA library computing the same uniquely-defined closeness coefficients; matched to &lt;1e-9. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mcda_topsis/generate_topsis_reference.py</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_topsis_reference.rs (closeness coefficients + ranking vs pymcdm to &lt;1e-9 on a fixed 4×3 benefit/cost matrix)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA compromise ranking (VIKOR)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>vikor</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>VlseKriterijumska Optimizacija — group-utility Sᵢ, individual-regret Rᵢ and the compromise index Qᵢ at strategy weight v=0.5, with the lower-is-better ranking</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pymcdm methods.VIKOR(v=0.5) — an independent third-party MCDA library computing the same uniquely-defined range-normalised S/R/Q aggregation; matched to &lt;1e-9. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mcda_vikor/generate_vikor_reference.py</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_vikor_reference.rs (Q index + ranking vs pymcdm to &lt;1e-9 on a fixed 4×3 benefit/cost matrix)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA outranking net-flow ranking (PROMETHEE II)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>promethee</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Preference Ranking Organization METHod — pairwise preference index with the six standard generalised-criterion shapes, positive/negative outranking flows and the complete net-flow ranking (usual criterion validated)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pymcdm methods.PROMETHEE_II(&#x27;usual&#x27;) — an independent third-party MCDA library computing the same uniquely-defined net outranking flow; matched to &lt;1e-9. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mcda_promethee/generate_promethee_reference.py. The thresholded (q/p/σ) preference shapes reduce to the same generalised-criterion algebra and stay property-checked</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_promethee_reference.rs (net outranking flow + ranking, usual criterion, vs pymcdm to &lt;1e-9 on a fixed 4×3 benefit/cost matrix)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA outranking choice kernel (ELECTRE I)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>electre</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>ELimination Et Choix Traduisant la REalité — concordance / discordance matrices, the concordance-and-non-veto dominance relation and the outranking choice kernel (all-benefit, sum-normalised-weight, single-global-scale convention)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pyDecision algorithm.electre_i (Valdecy Pereira) — an independent third-party multi-criteria decision library computing the same uniquely-defined concordance/discordance/dominance/kernel; matched element-for-element to &lt;1e-9. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mcda_electre/generate_electre_reference.py. The ĉ/d̂ threshold choices are analyst inputs (sensitivity stays MODELLED)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_electre_reference.rs (concordance, discordance, dominance matrices element-for-element + kernel/dominated set vs pyDecision to &lt;1e-9 on a fixed 4×3 all-benefit dataset)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA aggregation kernels (WASPAS / MOORA)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>waspas,<wbr> <wbr>mcda::<wbr>moora</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Weighted Aggregated Sum Product ASSessment (linear-normalised convex blend λ·WSM+(1−λ)·WPM at λ=0.5) and the MOORA ratio system (vector-normalised weighted benefit total minus weighted cost total) — the stability-hardened value blend and the signed ratio-system scorer</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pymcdm (methods.WASPAS + normalizations.linear_normalization, l=0.5; methods.MOORA ratio system, vector normalisation) — an independent, widely-used third-party Python MCDA library computing the same uniquely-defined aggregates; matched to &lt;1e-9. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mcda_waspas/ and tests/fixtures/mcda_moora/. Garbage-in-garbage-out on the inputs; the sensitivity/robustness layer stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_waspas_reference.rs (WASPAS preferences + ranking) and tests/mcda_moora_reference.rs (MOORA scores + ranking), each vs pymcdm to &lt;1e-9 on a fixed 4×3 benefit/cost decision matrix</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">MCDA proportional ranking (COPRAS)</span><code class="lg-mod">mcda::<wbr>copras</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>COmplex PRoportional ASsessment — column-sum-normalised benefit significance S⁺ plus the inverse-cost significance term, the relative significance Q and utility degree U = Q/max Q (best alternative = 1)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>pyDecision algorithm.copras_method (Valdecy Pereira) — an independent third-party MCDA library computing the same uniquely-defined COPRAS relative-significance/utility. pyDecision is used deliberately: pymcdm 1.4.0&#x27;s COPRAS collapses algebraically to the trivial S⁺+S⁻ and is not a faithful reference, whereas pyDecision implements the canonical Q = S⁺+(min(S⁻)·ΣS⁻)/(S⁻·Σ(min(S⁻)/S⁻)). Matched to &lt;1e-9; regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mcda_copras/generate_copras_reference.py</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mcda_copras_reference.rs (utility degrees + ranking vs pyDecision to &lt;1e-9 on a fixed 4×3 benefit/cost matrix)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">CUSUM change-detection latency &amp; ARL</span><code class="lg-mod">tpl <wbr>(Cusum),<wbr> <wbr>security</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Tabular-CUSUM detector worst-case detection latency (⌊h/(z−k)⌋+1) and out-of-control average run length, used by the timing-protection-level and spoof monitors</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Published tabular-CUSUM ARL: Siegmund (1985) Brownian-motion approximation (Hawkins &amp; Olwell 1998, eq. 3.7) cross-anchored to Montgomery, Introduction to Statistical Quality Control (Wiley) ARL tables for k=½, h∈{4,5}; deterministic latency matched exactly to a first-passage oracle. The composed TPL bound stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/timing_protection_level_under_spoofing_reference.rs (16 deterministic-latency cases EXACT vs a first-passage oracle; 8 ARL₁ cases, Monte-Carlo @60k trials vs the Siegmund approximation + published Montgomery…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Clock-holdover coast-variance &amp; threshold inversion</span><code class="lg-mod">holdover <wbr>(coast_<wbr>phase_<wbr>variance,<wbr> <wbr>holdover_<wbr>seconds)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Coast phase-error variance growth q_wf·t + q_rw·t³/3 + q_drift·t⁵/20 and its monotone inversion to a timing-error holdover threshold</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scipy 1.18 (BSD-3-Clause): linalg.expm computing the Van-Loan 1978 discrete process-noise Q₀₀ = ∫₀ᵗ ΦQcΦᵀds via Padé scaling-and-squaring on the 6×6 augmented matrix — an independent route that never sees kshana&#x27;s polynomial coefficients; plus optimize.brentq inverting the same monotone curve vs kshana&#x27;s bisection. The per-class red-noise floor figures (ClockClass) stay MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/gnss_denied_clock_holdover_reference.rs (27 cases vs scipy 1.18: 12 coast-variance vs linalg.expm Van-Loan Q₀₀ worst rel 1.1e-15; 7 holdover inversions vs optimize.brentq worst rel 1.5e-16)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Inverse-Simpson diversity kernel</span><code class="lg-mod">resilience::<wbr>diversity <wbr>(effective_<wbr>diversity)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Effective architectural diversity = inverse-Simpson / Hill-order-2 number 1/Σpᵢ² over per-independence-group source qualities (the diversity term in the resilience score)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scikit-bio 0.7.3 skbio.diversity.alpha.inv_simpson (McDonald et al., BSD-3-Clause) — an independent third-party library computing the uniquely-defined inverse-Simpson index; reproduced byte-for-byte. The DHS-RPCF scoring framework (weights/levels) built on top stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/resilience_diversity_reference.rs (17 cases: 14 vs scikit-bio inv_simpson + 3 pinned-zero boundary; worst |Δ| 0.0)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Azimuth-dependent skyline mask: visible set, azimuth and elevation, and DOP under the mask</span><code class="lg-mod">skyline <wbr>(Skyline::<wbr>cut_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>Skyline::<wbr>visible); <wbr>frames <wbr>(look_<wbr>angles,<wbr> <wbr>is_<wbr>visible_<wbr>skyline); <wbr>orbit <wbr>(UserPath,<wbr> <wbr>sky_<wbr>view,<wbr> <wbr>cofactor_<wbr>from_<wbr>los); <wbr>geometry <wbr>(sky_<wbr>geometry)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>skyline + frames::look_angles + geometry::sky_geometry: the geodetic (WGS-84) azimuth and elevation of a satellite from a ground site, an azimuth/elevation mask table linearly interpolated in azimuth with wrap at 360 deg, the visible set el &gt;= max(mask_deg, mask(az)), and the common-clock DOP and ENU-and-clock cofactor matrix of that set (the kernel behind orbit::dop). It is how the [skyline] section decides visibility in the gnss-sim, jamming, leo-pvt, leo-pass and nmea-scenario kinds and, with the ground-site user_site, in orbit and integrity</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0): a TopocentricFrame on a WGS-84 OneAxisEllipsoid for the look angles and an ElevationMask per table, run by xval/orekit-skyline/OrekitSkyline.java on committed Earth-fixed states (the propagation is not compared), with gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 (wheel metadata MIT) get_dop for the DOP and the ENU-and-clock DOP matrix of the sets Orekit finds visible. Pre-registered (xval/orekit-skyline/PREREGISTRATION.md, commit d94130d4) before either oracle was run on the comparison inputs; Orekit&#x27;s linear-with-wrap interpolation was confirmed beforehand on a synthetic probe. Scope: table masks, geodetic look angles, the masked visible set and its common-clock DOP and cofactor. NOT checked here: the canyon, sector and terrain reductions and the presets (their 360-point tables entered as inputs; rows &#x27;Street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a table&#x27; and &#x27;Skyline presets&#x27;), the per-system clock states and the error ellipse (row &#x27;Horizontal error ellipse and per-system cofactor from the geometry covariance&#x27;), the GMST rotation of the user_site ground site in orbit/integrity and its dating of each propagator to one instant (Orekit is given Earth-fixed states), and any real obstruction (line of sight only)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/skyline_orekit_oracle.rs (skyline_masks_agree_with_orekit_and_gnss_lib_py_as_pre_registered: 1173 look angles at six sites and four epochs, six mask tables, 142 visible sets; worst |d el| 1.3e-13 deg and |d az|…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a table</span><code class="lg-mod">skyline <wbr>(Canyon::<wbr>cut_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>Sector::<wbr>contains,<wbr> <wbr>TerrainPoint::<wbr>elevation_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>reduce_<wbr>terrain,<wbr> <wbr>SkylineCfg::<wbr>build)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>skyline: the street canyon in closed form (cut atan((h - a) / d) with d = (w/2) / abs(sin(az - s)), open ends for a finite length), sharp-edged sectors clockwise with wrap, and terrain points reduced to a table with the curvature drop d^2/(2 * 4/3 R); the skyline is their pointwise maximum with any table</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Internal: the closed forms are checked against hand-computed values (wall-top elevations of a stated street, the open-end condition, the curvature drop of a stated point). No external oracle: the design note scopes these reductions as MODELLED, and Orekit&#x27;s ElevationMask takes a table, so the comparison of row &#x27;Azimuth-dependent skyline mask: visible set, azimuth and elevation, and DOP under the mask&#x27; reads their 360-point tables as inputs and does not check them</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>skyline::tests (canyon_matches_hand_computed_wall_elevations; terrain_takes_the_curvature_drop; sectors_have_sharp_edges_and_wrap; a_preset_is_overridden_or_added_to_field_by_field; malformed_sections_are_refused)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Skyline presets (city street, harbour approach, fjord, container terminal)</span><code class="lg-mod">skyline <wbr>(PRESETS,<wbr> <wbr>preset,<wbr> <wbr>preset_<wbr>list)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>skyline::PRESETS: four REPRESENTATIVE geometries with the design note&#x27;s stated dimensions and a provenance line each: city-street (a canyon, street 0 deg, 20 m between 30 m walls, antenna 1.5 m), harbour-approach (sectors: open to seaward over 180 deg, quay buildings 8 deg over 90 deg, the breakwater 3 deg), fjord (terrain: walls 600 m above the antenna at 1.5 km either side of a north-south channel, curvature drop applied), container-terminal (sectors: stacks 12 deg over the landward half, crane booms 35 deg and 25 deg over two narrow sectors); overridable field by field</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>None: chosen and stated dimensions, not a survey of any street, port, fjord or terminal (the aspect ratio of the street is inside the 0.5-2 band of Oke (1988); the fjord is of the order of the west-Norwegian fjords; the stacks use the ISO 668 high-cube height). The test checks the presets against their own stated dimensions, a transcription check, which the validation rule does not count</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>skyline::tests (presets_carry_the_note_dimensions_and_provenance)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Horizontal error ellipse and per-system cofactor from the geometry covariance</span><code class="lg-mod">geometry <wbr>(sky_<wbr>geometry,<wbr> <wbr>ellipse); <wbr>orbit <wbr>(cofactor_<wbr>from_<wbr>los)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>geometry::sky_geometry: the ENU-and-clock cofactor (H^T H)^-1 with one receiver clock in common or one per system (the orbit::cofactor_from_los kernel), and the horizontal error ellipse from its east-north block (semi-axes k sigma sqrt(lambda), k = sqrt(-2 ln(1 - p)), the major axis&#x27;s azimuth from north)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>numpy 2.5.4 (BSD-3-Clause), xval/numpy-ellipse/ellipse.py: numpy.linalg.inv of G^T G and numpy.linalg.eigh of its horizontal block on the committed inputs, an independent numerical library performing the inverse and the eigen-decomposition by its own algorithms (standard P2). Pre-registered in xval/numpy-ellipse/PREREGISTRATION.md (commit 2dfc9c16) before the run; the driver&#x27;s output formatting was corrected once after the pre-registration (a numpy scalar written as np.float64(...)), with no change to the protocol or the tolerances. The validated claim is the linear algebra on those inputs; the Gaussian error model and the UERE are stated inputs, not a measured accuracy or an integrity bound</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/ellipse_numpy_oracle.rs (ellipse_and_cofactor_agree_with_numpy_as_pre_registered: 270 cases, the 142 committed visible sets of the skyline comparison under one common clock and under one clock per system; worst…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Sky geometry functions: visibility, DOP, covariance, error ellipse, Monte Carlo cloud</span><code class="lg-mod">geometry; <wbr>orbit <wbr>(cofactor_<wbr>from_<wbr>los,<wbr> <wbr>dop_<wbr>cov,<wbr> <wbr>dop); <wbr>surface <wbr>(sky_<wbr>geometry,<wbr> <wbr>sky_<wbr>monte_<wbr>carlo,<wbr> <wbr>skyline_<wbr>presets,<wbr> <wbr>skyline_<wbr>profile,<wbr> <wbr>skyplot_<wbr>svg)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>geometry (sky_geometry, sky_monte_carlo, skyline_presets, skyline_profile, skyplot_svg; CLI <code>kshana sky</code> and <code>kshana skyline</code>, Python and WASM functions of the same names, MCP <code>sky_geometry</code>): from a site, a time and constellation presets (or satellites as azimuth/elevation or ECEF), the satellites visible and masked under a skyline, GDOP/PDOP/HDOP/VDOP/TDOP, the unit-variance cofactor (H^T H)^-1 and the covariance in east-north-up with one receiver clock per system or one in common, the horizontal error ellipse (semi-axes k sigma sqrt(lambda), k = sqrt(-2 ln(1 - p)), orientation 0.5 atan2(2 Q_EN, Q_NN - Q_EE) from north), and n seeded least-squares errors under independent Gaussian range errors with their empirical covariance, RMS and 95 % containment. The cofactor is orbit::cofactor_from_los, the one kernel behind orbit::dop and orbit::dop_cov (dop is dop_cov&#x27;s reduction): no second DOP implementation</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The single-clock DOP factors rest on the kernel validated against gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 (row &#x27;GNSS geometry / dilution of precision (DOP)&#x27;); this row&#x27;s own checks are internal: sky_geometry reproduces orbit::dop and dop_cov on the same geometry, the ellipse reproduces an analytic 2x2 eigen-decomposition, ECEF and azimuth/elevation inputs agree, and the seeded cloud&#x27;s RMS and 95 % containment agree with the geometry. The ENU covariance with one clock per system and the ellipse are VALIDATED in row &#x27;Horizontal error ellipse and per-system cofactor from the geometry covariance&#x27; (numpy, P2); what stays MODELLED here is the composition: the preset propagation to a representative sky, the Monte Carlo cloud (checked against the geometry it samples, a self-consistency check) and the surfaces. The constellation presets give a representative sky, not a dated one; the Gaussian error model and the UERE are stated inputs, not a measured accuracy or an integrity bound</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>geometry::tests (ellipse_of_an_analytic_two_by_two_case; sky_geometry_equals_orbit_dop_on_the_same_visible_set; ecef_input_matches_azimuth_elevation_input; one_clock_per_system_adds_a_state_and_costs_geometry…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS-denied clock holdover</span><code class="lg-mod">holdover</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Closed-form coast-error growth + holdover-to-threshold; quantum-clock classes</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Multi-step clock_state covariance recursion (same-codebase cross-check); the underlying coast-variance &amp; holdover-inversion kernel is externally validated vs scipy (see &#x27;Clock-holdover coast-variance &amp; threshold inversion&#x27;). The per-class red-noise-floor holdover figures stay MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>holdover::tests (vs multi-step Kalman covariance recursion; white-FM exact; round-trip); coast-variance kernel externally validated in tests/gnss_denied_clock_holdover_reference.rs (vs scipy Van-Loan/brentq)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Measured clock device cards (held-out prediction)</span><code class="lg-mod">clock_<wbr>library <wbr>(card,<wbr> <wbr>condition,<wbr> <wbr>series),<wbr> <wbr>realdata::<wbr>clk,<wbr> <wbr>allan <wbr>(lag1_<wbr>noise_<wbr>id)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>clock_library: a device card (white phase, white, flicker and random-walk FM plus a linear frequency drift) fitted to one third of a named measured clock record after a logged conditioning pass (gaps, phase outliers, phase steps, bursts removed; frequency steps logged), weighted by the lag-1-identified noise-type degrees of freedom, and its predicted Allan deviation scored on the held-out two thirds; readers for RINEX and IGS clock files, BIPM per-laboratory files and Circular T Section 1; conversion of a card to the slot-timing noise model, the extended Kalman clock model and the spoofing monitor&#x27;s noise levels</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Measured records, held out: IGS final combined 30 s clocks of the 11 GPS Block IIF satellites over 2026-03-01 to 14 (a window no test had opened; IGS, open with attribution) and the JammerTest 2024 u-blox ZED-F9P receiver in its non-scored stationary sessions (GPL-3.0-or-later). D9 external comparison (pre-registered 14169982, every card&#x27;s predicted / measured Allan deviation within [1/1.5, 1.5] at its fitted averaging times), a finding (stays MODELLED): 10 of 11 GPS IIF cards are within the bar; G27 is optimistic at the two-hour scale (0.662 at 7680 s, worst factor 1.510), the periodic hour-scale error a power-law card does not carry. The receiver TCXO card is blocked: outside the logged transmissions the dataset&#x27;s other sessions leave 167 epochs against the 3600 required. Reported, not blind (records opened by earlier rows): the 5071A caesium card is within the bar (worst 1.070 up to 4096 s), the OCXO card is conservative and outside it (2.348 at 128 s; its noise floor changes during the record), and the Norcia strontium card fitted on the three shortest published points is within it at the two scored points (1.291, 1.405). Galileo passive hydrogen masers (no login-free source of final Galileo clocks reachable) and the Deep Space Atomic Clock (one open in-space value) are blocked. D9 round 2, the ZED-F9P receiver TCXO as a model class on 14 days of 12 static Wroclaw stations (Zenodo 6488497, CC BY 4.0; pre-registered 698f3842), a finding: with the registered pipeline 11 of 12 stations were not evaluable (RAIM rejected most ionosphere-free epochs) and BX14 failed; a disclosed re-run with a corrected pipeline (pre-registered cdfde420) passes 1 of 11 blind stations (BX12, 1.412; BX07 1.532 and BX13 1.542 just outside), because single-point receiver clocks from intermittent 30 s files are not a clean oscillator record</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/clock_library_device_cards_oracle.rs (gps_iif_cards_reproduce_the_recorded_finding; receiver_training_is_blocked_by_the_epoch_minimum; non_blind_cards_are_reported; pre-registered 14169982)…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Measured GPS Block IIF clock cards with per-revolution terms (held-out prediction)</span><code class="lg-mod">clock_<wbr>library <wbr>(card)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>clock_library::card::DeviceCard::fit_phase_periodic: a device card that carries the once- to four-per-revolution phase terms (period T/k, T = 43 082.05 s), fitted jointly with the quadratic by least squares on one third of a measured satellite clock record, with each term&#x27;s closed-form Allan contribution 4 A^2 sin^4(pi tau / P) / tau^2 in the predicted Allan deviation</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Measured records, held out: IGS final combined 30 s clocks of the 11 GPS Block IIF satellites over 2026-04-01 to 14 (a window no test had opened; IGS, open with attribution). D9 external comparison (pre-registered 698f3842, predicted / measured Allan deviation within [1/1.5, 1.5] at every fitted averaging time on every satellite), a finding (stays MODELLED): 9 of 11 cards are within the bar; G03 (1.947) and G25 (1.581) are optimistic between 60 s and 2000 s because their held-out records are noisier than their fit thirds (G25&#x27;s held-out part carries 16 gaps and 29 phase outliers), a change of the clock rather than a missing periodic term. Dropping the periodic terms from the prediction fails all 11 cards (1.77 to 4.96), so the terms carry the prediction</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/clock_library_periodic_cards_oracle.rs (periodic_cards_reproduce_the_recorded_finding; pre-registered 698f3842); clock_library::card::tests (a_periodic_card_carries_the_sinusoid_a_power_law_card_cannot)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Onboard clock state estimation</span><code class="lg-mod">clock_<wbr>state</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>3-state (phase/freq/drift) van-Loan Kalman clock, Joseph-stabilised</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>filterpy 1.4.5 KalmanFilter (R. Labbe, MIT), with F via scipy.linalg.expm and Q via the Van-Loan 1978 block-matrix — an independent reference implementation reproducing kshana&#x27;s full filter trajectory. Cross-implementation consistency: the clock physics / Allan calibration are not externally validated, so this stays MODELLED. 0.30 external comparison, a finding (stays MODELLED): on IGS final 30 s clocks of 11 GPS Block IIF satellites (2025-08-17 to 30), with Q fitted to the first-half ADEV and the filter scored on the held-out second half, the filter is consistent one step ahead (0.906 to 0.971 of epochs inside its 95 % band, bar 90 %) but over-confident at one hour on 9 of 11 clocks (0.486 to 0.829) and its innovation sums fall outside [2.4, 3.6] on 5. The fitted white-phase noise is zero on every satellite, and the hour-scale error (periodic terms, flicker FM) is not in the 3-state model. 0.30 round 2, an extended filter (flicker FM and once- and twice-per-revolution states) on fresh held-out IGS clocks of 2025-09-01 to 14 (pre-registered d8d18573), a finding (stays MODELLED): the one-step and one-hour consistency criteria hold on all 11 satellites (0.948 to 0.988 and 0.907 to 0.996), but the innovation-sum criterion fails on G24 (0.964) and G30 (1.929) against [2.4, 3.6]. D9 round 3, the round-2 extended filter, tuning and criteria unchanged after the frozen clock_library conditioning detector, on fresh IGS clocks of 2026-04-01 to 14 (pre-registered 698f3842), a finding (stays MODELLED): (a) one-step 0.941 to 0.968 and (c) triple-NIS mean 2.641 to 3.549 hold on all 11 satellites; (b) one hour fails on G09 (0.894) and G26 (0.876) against 0.90</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>clock_state::tests (analytic van-Loan Q; NEES; PSD positivity); tests/clock_state_reference.rs (full predict+update trajectory — state x and 3×3 covariance P over 1925 steps / 4 parameter sets vs filterpy 1.4.5; worst…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="signal"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Time-transfer error budgeting</span><code class="lg-mod">timetransfer,<wbr> <wbr>timetransfer_<wbr>adv</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Two-way/TWSTFT (Sagnac), GNSS common-view, PPP; link-jitter→range</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Sagnac magnitude checked against an authoritative PUBLISHED VALUE — N. Ashby, &#x27;Relativity in the GPS&#x27;, Living Reviews in Relativity 6:1 (2003), Eq. 1.29: an eastward equatorial circumnavigation accrues 207.4 ns (2ωA_E/c²); kshana reproduces 207.386 ns. Independently corroborated by RTKLIB 2.4.3 geodist() (Takasu, BSD-2-Clause), which carries the same 2Aω/c² geometry. The composite BIPM TWSTFT transponder/common-view/PPP budget has no external oracle, so the capability stays Modelled</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>timetransfer::tests (reciprocal cancellation; two-form Sagnac identity); tests/time_transfer_error_budgeting_reference.rs (equatorial-circumnavigation Sagnac = 207.386 ns vs the published Ashby 207.4 ns to &lt;0.05 ns…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="signal"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Nav-signal modulation &amp; code-tracking analysis</span><code class="lg-mod">navsignal</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>BPSK-R/BOC PSD, spectral-separation κ, Gabor bandwidth, DLL jitter, multipath</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>GPS C/A Gold cross/auto-correlation matched EXACTLY (integer ±65/−1/63) against independent IS-GPS-200 code generation; BPSK-R(1)/BOC(1,1) PSD shape vs an independent scipy periodogram. The modulation/SSC/DLL closed forms (Betz 2001 / Kaplan &amp; Hegarty) remain analytic, so the row stays MODELLED — but the code-correlation sub-claim is externally matched. 0.30 round 2, against the printed values of Betz 2001 and Hein et al. 2006 (pre-registered 1181ce1d, half a printed unit), a finding (stays MODELLED): all 45 Table 1 spectral cells, the 1 GHz correlation values, the MBOC interference figures and 2 of 3 Fig. 17 offsets agree; the 24 MHz first side lobes, the BOC(8,4) Fig. 17 offset and every Figs. 18-21 worst-case multipath bias (11 to 15 % low) do not. Kshana and an independent NumPy evaluation of the pre-registered reading agree, so the gap lies in that reading or in an unstated detail of the paper; cause unresolved</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>navsignal::tests (BPSK self-SSC = 2/3R_c; unit-area PSD; DLL); tests/nav_signal_modulation_code_tracking_reference.rs (GPS C/A Gold cross/auto-correlation exact-integer match vs independent IS-GPS-200 code generation…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">GPS L1 C/A spreading-code generation</span><code class="lg-mod">sdr</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>G1/G2 LFSR C/A-code generator reproducing the IS-GPS-200 published first-10-chip octal verification vectors for PRN 1–9 (plus 1023-chip length, 512-ones balance, three-valued periodic autocorrelation)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>IS-GPS-200 Table 3-Ia &#x27;Code Phase Assignments&#x27; — the authoritative US-Government public-domain published first-10-chip OCTAL verification vectors (GPS Directorate, navcen.uscg.gov); kshana&#x27;s own G1/G2 LFSR regenerates every PRN 1–9 vector exactly. The downstream modulation/SSC/DLL closed forms stay Modelled (separate row)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>sdr::code_tests (ca_first_ten_chips_match_is_gps_200_octal — PRN 1–9 first-10-chips reproduce IS-GPS-200 Table 3-Ia octals 1440/1620/1710/1744/1133/1455/1131/1454/1626 exactly; ca_code_has_1023_chips_and_is_balanced…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Quantum inertial sensor performance</span><code class="lg-mod">inertial::<wbr>quantum_<wbr>imu</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Cold-atom interferometer accelerometer from first principles (k_eff·T², QPN)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Published CAI primary-paper numeric vectors (Cheinet 2008 transfer function; Peters/Freier sensitivity): k_eff·T² matched exactly, shot-noise ASD a one-sided floor within ~2× of each published instrument (real devices carry technical noise above the quantum floor). A bracket, not parity. 0.30 round 2 (pre-registered e7de532a), a finding (stays MODELLED): the QPN-only rotation noise against the measured Gauguet et al. 2009 Fig. 14 points gives ratios 0.695 to 0.788 at the three admitted points (0.751 at the operating point), missing the pre-registered 30 % bar by 0.5 points; the QPN floor sits 21 to 31 % below the measured noise, as expected of a model without laser and detection noise. The gradiometer composition matches the Janvier et al. 2022 QPN model line (ratio 1.013 to 1.017, Reference). The measured test cannot discriminate a sqrt(2) composition error; only the unequal-contrast Janvier line can</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>quantum_imu::tests (k_eff; Mach-Zehnder T²; Freier-2016 floor bracket); tests/quantum_inertial_sensor_reference.rs (transfer function |H(ω)|, k_eff·T² and shot-noise ASD vs published Cheinet 2008 / Peters / Freier…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Quantum inertial sensor fringe-ambiguity / dynamic range</span><code class="lg-mod">inertial::<wbr>quantum_<wbr>imu</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Mach–Zehnder fringe-ambiguity dynamic range: the 2π-periodic fringe readout sets a maximum unambiguous specific force a_max=π/(k_eff·T²), and the unambiguous range in resolution cells a_max/σ_a=π/σ_Φ is independent of the optical scale factor — the T² sensitivity gain costs unambiguous range in exact lockstep (interrogation time trades resolution for range, leaving the cell count fixed by the readout phase noise)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Self-consistency of the interferometer fringe model: the half-fringe edge, the 2π-periodic aliasing structure, and the scale-factor cancellation in the range/resolution ratio are closed-form algebraic identities checked against the engine&#x27;s own Mach–Zehnder phase and sensitivity functions — internal-consistency checks, NOT an external dataset, so the row stays InternalConsistency. MODELLED ideal three-pulse fringe-ambiguity; no wavefront-aberration or contrast-loss bounds on the unambiguous range</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>quantum_imu::tests (a_max sits at the ±π half-fringe edge with the 1/T² range scaling; wrapped-phase recovery is exact inside [−a_max,a_max] and aliases by exactly 2·a_max outside it; the unambiguous dynamic range…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Quantum inertial dead-reckoning resilience</span><code class="lg-mod">inertial::<wbr>quantum_<wbr>imu <wbr>(QuantumNavBudget)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Composed bias + scale-factor + VRW + stability-decay position budget over holdover</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Independent numpy Monte-Carlo SDE integration of double-integrated white-acceleration noise (validates the analytic VRW variance by a genuinely independent algorithm) + a Groves 2013 published-value anchor for the bias/scale-factor terms; the CAI device numbers quantify partner hardware and stay MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>budget_tests (bias vs AccelModel integrator; VRW vs analytic integral); tests/quantum_inertial_dead_reckoning_reference.rs (VRW vs an independent numpy Monte-Carlo double-integration of white-acceleration noise, worst…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS/INS sensor fusion</span><code class="lg-mod">fusion <wbr>(gnss_<wbr>ins_<wbr>ekf,<wbr> <wbr>tightly_<wbr>coupled,<wbr> <wbr>ukf,<wbr> <wbr>coupled)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>15-state error-state EKF, loosely coupled (validated on a real IMU/GNSS drive against NaveGo); a tightly coupled pseudorange/Doppler UKF and a coupled clock+position filter (consistency-only, outside the validated claim)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NaveGo v1.4 (R. Gonzalez, LGPL-3.0, run as a tool under GNU Octave, never linked): its loosely coupled ins_gnss on its own real Ekinox IMU/GNSS land-vehicle drive, both solutions scored against the Ekinox reference trajectory. Both filters read the same 20 Hz float32 input, and Kshana&#x27;s configuration is mapped mechanically from the dataset&#x27;s published parameters. Kshana&#x27;s horizontal and vertical position RMS and its position-innovation RMS are within 1.2x of NaveGo&#x27;s (measured 0.912, 0.940 and 0.861) and its mean normalised innovation is at most 1.5 (measured 0.395: about 2.5x under-confident, given the dataset&#x27;s stated GNSS sigmas); tolerances fixed before the comparison. Scope: GNSS is present at 5 Hz for the whole drive, so no outage or coast is tested and the check catches gross filter defects rather than fine tuning (lever-arm-corrected raw fixes would land near the vertical bar); the lever-arm transform is computed in the test harness. The tightly coupled UKF and the coupled clock+position filter keep their filterpy 1.4.5 consistency checks (linear posteriors to 2.4e-12) and stay outside the validated claim</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>fusion::tests (UKF==linear-KF identity; outage coast; NEES); tests/gnss_ins_sensor_fusion_reference.rs (50 cases vs filterpy 1.4.5: linear EKF loose/tight + coupled-PNT posteriors to ≤2.4e-12; UKF 40-epoch run worst…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="signal"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS-denied jamming resilience</span><code class="lg-mod">jamming</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Geometry J/S link budget, anti-jam C/N₀, per-satellite loss-of-lock</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Anti-jam C/N₀ link-budget equation cross-checked against an independent numpy re-derivation (shares the same closed form → InternalConsistency) plus a real-JammerTest-2024 C/N₀ degradation characterisation. 0.30 round 2, blocked (stays MODELLED): the measured JammerTest 2024 C/N0 drop against a link-budget prediction (pre-registered 6b2f6240, within 3 dB) was not run because the F8.1 antenna position and pointing at Bleik and the L1 share of the multi-band 1.6.4 ramp power are not documented in the test catalogue or the official log</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>jamming::tests (broadband_q_equals_the_spectral_separation_closed_form and cw_q_equals_the_spectral_separation_closed_form: the Q table, CW and narrowband 1, broadband and swept 2, pinned to the navsignal closed forms…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Spoofing detection</span><code class="lg-mod">spoof,<wbr> <wbr>spoof_<wbr>detect,<wbr> <wbr>spoof_<wbr>monitors</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Clock-aided χ², RAIM, AGC, SQM fused per-epoch security FoM</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>TEXBAT scenario parameters (Humphreys 2012) — characterisation, not pinned vectors. 0.30 external comparison, a finding (stays MODELLED): on JammerTest 2024 (Bleik; GPL-3.0 data) the observable-level monitors (clock-aided chi-square, RAIM, solve failure) raised no alarm within 10 s of any of the 8 evaluable published spoofing onsets (where the pre-onset window was clean, the first alarm came 10.2 to 39 s after the published slot start), and three pre-onset windows carried false alarms from the clock monitor (81, 64 and 173 epochs). The first observable effect was a loss of dual-frequency GPS tracking, not a RAIM inconsistency. The published onsets are minute-resolution schedule slot starts; that they precede the RF capture is an interpretation, not a measurement. 0.30 round 2, against the organisers official JammerTest 2024 log (second resolution; pre-registered 6b2f6240 with a Hadamard-calibrated three-state clock monitor without latching), a finding (stays MODELLED): the monitor alarms within 10 s at 4 of 10 logged onsets; at the other six it is 18 to 211 s late or raises pre-onset clock false alarms (1 at 2.1.4, 85 at 2.3.15). D9 round 3 (a receiver card from the JammerTest unit&#x27;s other sessions, pre-registered 14169982) blocked: 167 training epochs against 3600. D9 rounds 4 and 4b (noise levels from a u-blox ZED-F9P model-class card, Wroclaw 2021, pre-registered 698f3842; 4b a disclosed re-run with a corrected extraction, cdfde420), a finding (stays MODELLED): zero pre-onset false alarms at all 10 logged onsets and 6 of 10 within 10 s; 2.1.1, 2.3.5, 2.3.10 and 2.6.1 stay late (+211, +23, +18, +51 s), unchanged by the clock noise level</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/spoof_texbat_validation.rs (TEXBAT parameter characterisation); tests/spoof_detection_jammertest_oracle.rs::monitors_against_the_published_onsets_reproduce_the_recorded_disagreement (pins the finding)…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Timing Protection Level under spoofing</span><code class="lg-mod">tpl</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Closed-form bound on worst-case undetected time error = monitor floor + oscillator coast-σ over CUSUM detection latency, reported as a red-noise-floor band</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Composes Validated primitives (allan/holdover van-Loan, security floor); calibrated on JammerTest 2024 scenario 2.1.1 (~1.01 ms real served-time pull vs ≤51 ns claimed). Bridge over Validated parts — not itself an external validation. 0.30 external comparison, a finding (stays MODELLED): on the JammerTest 2024 spoofing scenarios the measured undetected served-time error exceeds the nominal TPL at 3 of 8 detected onsets: 184.3 vs 34.6 ns (2.1.4), 295.7 vs 159.3 ns (2.3.5) and 143.0 vs 69.0 ns (2.3.11); three of the five inside are trivial (a latched pre-onset false alarm). Likely cause: the coast term from a 60 s calibration with zero drift noise does not cover the receiver TCXO&#x27;s frequency wander over 15 to 39 s. The measured error is Kshana&#x27;s own single-point clock estimate against a line fitted to its own pre-onset estimates, so even agreement would not have been fully independent. 0.30 round 2, against the official JammerTest 2024 log onsets and the measured receiver time error (pre-registered 6b2f6240), a finding (stays MODELLED): at 4 of 10 logged onsets the undetected error exceeds the drift-state TPL (634.7 against 458.5 ns at 2.1.1, 232.6 against 16.2 ns at 2.3.5, 41.3 against 6.0 ns at 2.3.10, 218.6 against 47.4 ns at 2.6.3)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tpl::tests (closed-form oracles + CUSUM); examples/tpl_jammertest.rs (JammerTest 2024 real-spoof calibration); tests/tpl_jammertest_coverage_oracle.rs::tpl_against_measured_time_error_reproduces_the_recorded_disagreemen…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Cislunar mission analysis</span><code class="lg-mod">cr3bp</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>CR3BP STM + single-shooting differential corrector; L2 southern NRHO</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NASA/JPL Three-Body Periodic Orbit Database (SSD, periodic_orbits.api; Earth–Moon L2 Southern halo family, Howell/Davis methodology) — externally-published period T, Jacobi C and perpendicular-crossing initial conditions; kshana&#x27;s single-shooting STM corrector, seeded with the catalog IC and perturbed, converges back onto the catalog members</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>cr3bp::tests (STM vs finite-diff); tests/cislunar_mission_analysis_reference.rs (5 JPL L2-S NRHO members: the 9:2 + 4 neighbours; worst |ΔC| 1.5e-5, |ΔT|/T 9.6e-5, perilune 0.7 km in JPL length units)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Alternative / complementary PNT</span><code class="lg-mod">altpnt,<wbr> <wbr>mapmatch,<wbr> <wbr>gravimeter,<wbr> <wbr>igrf</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Gravity-map matching, terrain-referenced (TERCOM/SITAN), magnetic anomaly</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>IGRF-14 coefficients; first-principles matched-filter CRLB. The real-field gravity benchmark&#x27;s field is externally checked (row &#x27;Gravity-anomaly synthesis from a spherical-harmonic model&#x27;), but its navigation outcome has no external oracle</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/alternative_complementary_pnt_reference.rs (map-matching CRLB; IGRF-14 field); mapmatch::tests; gravimeter::tests; igrf::tests; tests/gravity_map_real_field.rs (the 60-min GPS-denied benchmark on the real EGM2008…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Gravity-anomaly synthesis from a spherical-harmonic model</span><code class="lg-mod">gravity_<wbr>sh,<wbr> <wbr>altpnt::<wbr>maps,<wbr> <wbr>gravimeter</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Gravity anomaly in spherical approximation (Barthelmes STR09/02 eq. 126, ICGEM gravity_anomaly_sa) from fully normalised coefficients against a closed-form level ellipsoid (WGS84/GRS80), with the zero-degree term, gridded for the gravity-map matcher</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>ICGEM Calculation Service (GFZ Potsdam), an independent implementation: EGM2008 truncated at degree 70, gravity_anomaly_sa, WGS84, tide-free, zero-degree term, h = 0. The service output carries no explicit licence, so the grids are fetched at test time with a pinned SHA-256 (tools/fetch_icgem_grid.sh) and only ten values are cited with attribution</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/icgem_gravity_anomaly_oracle.rs (egm2008_n70_matches_cited_icgem_values in CI on ten cited ICGEM nodes; egm2008_n70_gravity_anomaly_sa_matches_icgem over the full fetched 0.25-deg grids of Chad 9-15N 19-25E and…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">SRTM digital-elevation reader on real terrain</span><code class="lg-mod">altpnt::<wbr>terrain</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Hand-rolled SRTM .hgt parser (16-bit big-endian, north-row-first, void-aware) + bilinear sampler reading a real public-domain DEM tile and resolving a documented survey benchmark</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>NASA/USGS SRTM v3 (1-arc-second) N36W117 tile — US-Government PUBLIC-DOMAIN elevation data from the AWS elevation-tiles-prod open mirror, decimated to 6-arc-second and committed under tests/fixtures/terrain/ (see NOTICE.md), plus full-resolution crops of N36W117 and N36W119 cross-checked node-for-node against GDAL&#x27;s independent SRTMHGT reader and the Mount Whitney (4421 m) and Badwater (−86 m) survey benchmarks. The documented Badwater Basin benchmark (−86 m, lowest in North America) anchors the geo-referenced read. The terrain-matching/TERCOM nav-fix that consumes the DEM stays Modelled (separate Alternative/complementary PNT row)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/terrain_nav_validation.rs (real_srtm_committed_badwater_tile_reads_real_relief — the committed 6-arc-second decimation of the public-domain SRTM v3 N36W117 tile places Badwater Basin, the lowest point in North…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Reproducibility &amp; software assurance</span><code class="lg-mod">report,<wbr> <wbr>scenario; <wbr>CI <wbr>(golden/<wbr>determinism/<wbr>SBOM)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Deterministic, scenario-hashed, SBOM + cross-platform golden gates</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>SBOM conformance to the official CycloneDX 1.5 JSON Schema (+ valid SPDX identifiers) — an external published standard, zero validation errors over the full dependency graph; the FoM-determinism / byte-reproducibility part remains a pinned self-consistency check, so the row stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/golden.rs, tests/determinism.rs, tests/cross_platform_golden.rs; tests/reproducibility_software_assurance_reference.rs (the generated SBOM validates with zero errors against the official CycloneDX 1.5 JSON Schema…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">AI/ML RF-impairment detection evaluation (13494)</span><code class="lg-mod">impairment_<wbr>eval</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Labelled synthetic impairment corpus + detector-agnostic ROC/AUC/confusion/Pfa-Pmd harness; leakage guard, stratified split, distribution-shift (in- vs out-of-regime) optimism report. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>impairment-eval</code> scenario kind (scenarios/impairment-eval.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Closed-form AUC bounds (Mann–Whitney) + a perfect-oracle detector; corpus is SYNTHETIC (parameter-grounded, not field/IQ)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>impairment_eval::tests (AUC perfect=1/identical=0.5/tie=0.125, ROC monotone, fused&gt;0.8, per-class layer separation, leakage guard, reproducible corpus, distribution-shift flags optimism); dominance_demonstrators…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">AI/ML RF-impairment optimism-gap study &amp; ID-only gap predictor</span><code class="lg-mod">impairment_<wbr>study,<wbr> <wbr>impairment_<wbr>ml,<wbr> <wbr>eval_<wbr>stats</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Controlled synthetic study of the in-distribution→out-of-distribution AUC optimism gap across published-method and learned (logistic-regression / one-hidden-layer MLP) detectors: per-class scaling-law trends (Spearman ρ + slope on 1−severity) and an ID-only ridge predictor that estimates the gap from in-distribution diagnostics alone, scored leave-one-detector-out and leave-one-class-out. Reproducible via <code>cargo run --release --example optimism_study</code></dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand-derived statistics vs closed forms (binormal AUC Φ(d&#x27;/√2), DeLong variance, tied-rank Spearman, exact OLS recovery) + leave-one-out CV against the predict-the-mean baseline. Corpus is SYNTHETIC (parameter-grounded, never field/IQ) and the optimism gap is a synthetic→synthetic severity shift, NOT a sim-to-field result</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>impairment_study::tests (per-class oracle AUC≈1, learned optimism gap&gt;0, grid shape + bootstrap CI brackets the mean + positive scaling trend, ID features finite, gap predictor beats predict-the-mean under BOTH…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Quantum-vs-classical PNT trade &amp; GNSS-denied resilience (13503)</span><code class="lg-mod">quantum_<wbr>trade</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Measured-ADEV ingestion (NNLS), trade table (timing/inertial holdover + benefit), resilience-vs-time envelope; floor caveat carried on the artifact. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>quantum-trade</code> scenario kind (scenarios/quantum-trade.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The measured-ADEV→PSD fit (NNLS) kernel is matched to scipy.optimize.nnls (tests/scipy_reference.rs / tests/quantum_vs_classical_pnt_trade_reference.rs) — an independent external kernel; but the trade NUMBERS quantify (never validate) a partner clock/CAI, so the trade itself stays MODELLED, no validation halo</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>quantum_trade::tests (ADEV round-trip recovery, NNLS non-negativity, floor-caveat present/absent, benefit&gt;1, monotone envelope + alt-PNT bound); dominance_demonstrators (measured-ADEV is data-driven not floor-assumed…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Space-weather environment &amp; activity-driven thermospheric density</span><code class="lg-mod">space_<wbr>weather</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Solar/geomagnetic indices (definitional Kp↔ap table), the Jacchia-1971 exospheric temperature, and the Jacchia 1971 thermospheric density (SAO Special Report 332: static diffusion profiles reproducing its Table 7, with the diurnal, geomagnetic, semiannual, seasonal-latitudinal and helium variations) against the static USSA76 atmosphere. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>space-weather</code> scenario kind (scenarios/space-weather.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Definitional Kp↔ap table + Jacchia-1971 exospheric-temperature closed form (matched to &lt;1 K vs the published anchors, tests/space_weather_reference.rs); the density is characterised against pymsis NRLMSISE-00 (an independent NRL model): the 800 km solar-cycle swing is 10.4x against NRLMSISE-00 13.6x. 0.30 revision: the calibrated first-order activity factor was replaced by Jacchia 1971 (default scenario density at 400 km 4.176e-12 -&gt; 6.929e-12 kg/m3; reference_exospheric_temperature_k removed, mean_exospheric_temperature_k added). 0.30 round 2, measured TOLEOS CHAMP/GRACE/GRACE-FO accelerometer densities (ESA Swarm DISC; pre-registered bb8756e3), a finding (stays MODELLED): orbit averages at 400 to 500 km are within a factor 2 at solar maximum outside storms (CHAMP 2001 521/521, GRACE-A 2002 120/120, GRACE-FO 2024 526/547), but Jacchia 1971 is about 1.7x too dense in the 2008 solar minimum (GRACE-A 412/544 within a factor 2), so the density is not validated</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>space_weather::tests (Kp↔ap exact at grid points + round-trip + monotone, daily-Ap mean, exospheric-T vs published solar-min/mean/max + storm increment anchors, density unity-at-reference, solar-cycle swing in the…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">CCSDS OEM interoperability (GMAT/Orekit/STK ephemeris import)</span><code class="lg-mod">oem</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>CCSDS 502.0 OEM importer (parse_oem), tolerant of COMMENT lines / extra metadata keywords / covariance blocks and the exact inverse of the writer; round-trip + external-file ingest with a velocity-consistency check. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>oem-interop</code> scenario kind (scenarios/oem-interop.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Independent third-party CCSDS-502 parser <code>oem</code> 0.4.5 (MIT, https://pypi.org/project/oem/) — a separate codebase that decodes kshana&#x27;s emitted EME2000/UTC OEM byte-identically (24 states across 2 fixtures, pos/vel Δ = 0) and whose strict reader confirms the metadata tokens; kshana&#x27;s parser likewise agrees with it on a vendored external OEM. Two honest interop findings (the oem library rejects kshana&#x27;s per-satellite multi-segment convention and its multi-entry covariance lines) are documented in the test — so this validates the conformant single-object interchange, not full CCSDS-502 conformance of every kshana variant</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/ccsds_oem_interop_reference.rs (24 states / 2 fixtures decoded byte-identically by the independent oem parser, pos/vel Δ = 0); oem::tests (parse an external-tool OEM with extra keywords/comments/covariance…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Launch-window &amp; ascent geometry (mission analysis)</span><code class="lg-mod">launch</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Two-body launch azimuth(s) (sin Az = cos i / cos lat), minimum reachable inclination, circular velocity, Earth-rotation eastward bonus, dogleg plane-change Δv and daily opportunities, and, given an epoch and IERS finals2000A rows, the site speed about the true pole (launch::site_rotation_speed_at). Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>launch-window</code> scenario kind (scenarios/launch-window.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Closed-form spherical-trig launch geometry vs published worked-example anchors (Vallado, Fundamentals of Astrodynamics 4th ed., Algorithm 37 launch-azimuth + Ch.6 plane-change; tests/launch_window_ascent_geometry_reference.rs). These re-use the same closed form kshana implements (a published-value parity / transcription check, InternalConsistency); MODELLED two-body, no rotating-Earth velocity-triangle / ascent / drag-loss model. 0.30 external comparison against Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0), a finding (stays MODELLED): the geometry agrees to rounding (inclination read back from the burnout azimuth over 158 cases, worst 2.6e-15 rad; minimum inclination, circular velocity and dogleg delta-v to 3e-16), but the Earth-rotation site speed misses the pre-registered 1e-6 relative at 62.9 deg latitude (1.12e-6). The gap grows as tan(lat), consistent with a polar-motion pole offset of about 5.8e-7 rad in Orekit&#x27;s Earth frame that the spherical ω·R·cos(lat) omits. The claim was not narrowed before the run, so the row cannot promote on this comparison. 0.30 round 2 (pre-registered 60d7f773): with an epoch and IERS rows, the true-pole site speed (scenario field site_rotation_speed_true_pole_m_s) agrees with Orekit within 1e-6 at 24 sites over four longitudes (worst 4.9e-8), but the nominal no-epoch speed the scenario emits by default as the Earth-rotation bonus still misses by 1.12e-6, so the row stays MODELLED pending a decision on the default output</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>launch::tests (due-east launch reaches i=latitude, KSC→ISS = textbook 45°, polar = N/S, i&lt;lat unreachable, 465 m/s equatorial bonus, plane-change 10° ≈ 1.34 km/s + 180° = 2v, daily-opportunity counts, scenario…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Ballistic re-entry corridor (Allen–Eggers)</span><code class="lg-mod">reentry</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Peak deceleration (ballistic-coefficient-independent), velocity + altitude at peak-g, and peak-heating velocity for an exponential-atmosphere ballistic entry. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>reentry</code> scenario kind (scenarios/reentry.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Closed-form Allen–Eggers analytic entry, additionally cross-checked vs a scipy 1.18 solve_ivp (DOP853) numerical integration of the SAME drag-only entry ODE (tests/ballistic_re_entry_corridor_reference.rs, 36 cases, worst a_max rel 2.9e-9) — a numeric-integral-vs-own-analytic-form check, so still InternalConsistency, NOT an external validation. MODELLED ballistic (no lift), no aerothermal/TPS — heating output is a velocity, not a heat-flux. 0.30 external comparison, a finding (stays MODELLED): against the Stardust SRC entry reconstruction (Desai and Qualls, NASA NTRS 20080008567), the Allen–Eggers peak deceleration for the printed inertial entry state (12.9 km/s, -8.2 deg, H 7200 m) is 61.8 g against the best-estimated-trajectory 32.89 g, +88 % against a 15 % tolerance: the constant-flight-path-angle, no-gravity solution roughly doubles the peak of a shallow, faster-than-escape entry. Stardust carried no accelerometer, so the reconstruction is a trajectory estimate. 0.30 round 2, blocked (stays MODELLED): an integrated planar point-mass solution (reentry::simulate_planar_entry, US76, inverse-square gravity, curvature) agrees with the Stardust and Genesis best-estimated trajectories within 15 % (+11.3 %, +5.6 %), but those maxima are agency simulation outputs, not measurements, so this is a cross-check only. The accelerometer comparison (pre-registered 18863477) is blocked on a readable copy of the Hayabusa2 REMM analysis (doi 10.57350/jesa.16)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>reentry::tests (peak-g independent of ballistic coefficient + physical g-band, grows with steeper γ / faster entry, peak-g velocity = V_e·e^(−1/2) and peak-heating = V_e·e^(−1/6) faster, peak-g altitude physical +…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">EO payload footprint &amp; coverage geometry</span><code class="lg-mod">eo_<wbr>payload <wbr>(j2_<wbr>node_<wbr>rate,<wbr> <wbr>j2_<wbr>nodal_<wbr>period,<wbr> <wbr>ground_<wbr>track_<wbr>spacing_<wbr>equator_<wbr>j2,<wbr> <wbr>sun_<wbr>synchronous_<wbr>inclination)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>SMAD space-triangle geometry: Earth angular radius, swath width, nadir GSD, maximum off-nadir access and circular period, plus the first-order J2 nodal period (eo_payload::j2_nodal_period) and the equatorial ground-track spacing R_e (omega_E - dOmega/dt) T_nodal (eo_payload::ground_track_spacing_equator_j2) with a contiguous-coverage flag, for an orbit altitude, inclination and sensor FOV/IFOV. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>eo-coverage</code> scenario kind (scenarios/eo-coverage.toml); its default inclination is the sun-synchronous one from eo_payload::sun_synchronous_inclination, a convenience default that is not part of the validated claim</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) and GeographicLib 2.1 (MIT), both run as separate programs, pre-registered (80513191) before the engine change and any oracle run: sphere quantities within 1e-9 relative (worst: angular radius 5.4e-16, swath 7.0e-13, access 1.9e-14), period 1e-12 (1.6e-16), nadir GSD 1e-7 (2.9e-10), the J2 nodal period and node spacing within 0.1 % of an Orekit numerical J2 propagation with a node detector (1.2e-5 and 6.7e-5), the contiguous flag exact (168/168), and against the WGS-84 ellipsoid the limb within 0.3 deg (0.186 deg) and the maximum ground range within 0.5 % (3.2e-3). Removing the nodal regression term fails 35 of 42 node points. MODELLED scope: spherical-Earth footprint; no radiometry, MTF, atmosphere, jitter or glint; the sun-synchronous default inclination is not compared. The earlier Skyfield/SGP4 cross-check is kept as a regression. 0.30 revision: the spacing now carries the J2 nodal regression (default scenario 2756.37 -&gt; 2752.17 km)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>eo_payload::tests (angular radius 64° at 700 km + shrinks with altitude, nadir→zenith/zero-range, horizon→ε=0/max central angle, past-horizon errors, swath grows with FOV / GSD with altitude, ~2750 km node spacing…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">CCSDS Space Packet (133.0) TM/TC framing</span><code class="lg-mod">space_<wbr>packet</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>CCSDS 133.0-B Space Packet primary-header encode/decode (version/type/sec-hdr/APID/seq-flags/count/data-length) + a framing scenario. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>space-packet</code> scenario kind (scenarios/space-packet.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>spacepackets 0.32.0 (us-irs/spacepackets-py, R. Mueller, Apache-2.0) — an independent third-party implementation of CCSDS 133.0-B-2; kshana&#x27;s encode_packet/decode_packet matched byte-exact (the 6-octet primary header for 33 cases + the full encoded packet for 12)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>space_packet::tests (header bits match the CCSDS-133 layout, encode→decode round-trips all fields, out-of-range/truncated rejected); tests/ccsds_space_packet_reference.rs (33 cases vs spacepackets 0.32.0, incl. 12…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">CCSDS Space Packet secondary header, packet error control and segmentation</span><code class="lg-mod">space_<wbr>packet <wbr>(TimeCode,<wbr> <wbr>SecondaryHeader,<wbr> <wbr>packet <wbr>error <wbr>control,<wbr> <wbr>segmentation <wbr>and <wbr>reassembly,<wbr> <wbr>stream <wbr>decoding)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>CCSDS 133.0-B-2 Space Packet extensions beside the primary header: a secondary header carrying a CCSDS 301.0-B time code (CUC or CDS, explicit or implicit P-field) and ancillary data, the CRC-16 packet error control field, segmentation of a large user-data unit and its reassembly, stream decoding with sequence-gap and order checks, and PUS-TM packets. The primary-header output is byte-identical to before</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Pre-registered (1bd991e9) before any comparison; four independent implementations, none GPL, nothing vendored: ccsdspy 2.0.1 (BSD-3-Clause) decodes 34 Kshana-written packets in 5 streams (primary header, P-field, coarse and fine time, CDS fields, ancillary data, user data, PEC) and both 1000-octet segmented units are rebuilt from its user data with 0 mismatches, and it flags the dropped and the swapped packet streams that Kshana rejects; the CRC of 8 messages and 57 PEC fields equals CPython binascii.crc_hqx and spacepackets-rs 0.18.0 (check value 0x29B1); 66 CUC and CDS time-code cases equal spacepackets-rs 0.18.0 write and read octet for octet (3 CDS cases also spacepackets-py 0.32.0); 4 spacepackets-py 0.32.0 PusTm packets are byte-identical to Kshana&#x27;s. Not covered: agency-epoch time codes (7 cases rest on unit tests), TC transfer frames and any link layer below the packet</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/space_packet_ext_oracle.rs (five pre-registered bars: CRC, time codes, streams, gap and order, PUS TM; zero mismatches); space_packet::tests</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">SPICE SPK export (types 9 and 13)</span><code class="lg-mod">spk_<wbr>writer <wbr>(SpkSegment,<wbr> <wbr>to_<wbr>bytes,<wbr> <wbr>from_<wbr>bytes,<wbr> <wbr>eval,<wbr> <wbr>et_<wbr>from_<wbr>label,<wbr> <wbr>oem_<wbr>to_<wbr>spk_<wbr>segments)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Writer and reader of NAIF SPICE SPK kernels of types 9 (Lagrange, unequal time steps) and 13 (Hermite, unequal time steps), with the DAF layout, segment descriptors and epoch directories, and the export of a CCSDS OEM to an SPK kernel (UTC or TDB epochs to ephemeris time)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0, leapseconds kernel naif0012.tls, pre-registered (75bc61d9) before SPICE read any Kshana kernel: SPICE&#x27;s inventory of Kshana&#x27;s four kernels is exact; spkpvn and spkgeo at 5 516 epochs and 690 chain evaluations equal Kshana&#x27;s own evaluation bit for bit and stay inside the bar at every node (worst 0.022 of it); Kshana reads SPICE-written kernels bit-identically; for the same inputs Kshana&#x27;s kernels are byte-identical to SPICE&#x27;s (0 differing bytes in four files); OEM epochs convert to ephemeris time within 6.0e-8 s of str2et (bar 1e-6 s) and SPICE evaluates the OEM export inside the bar (worst 0.060). Synthetic Kepler arcs: nothing about physical accuracy. Other SPK types are not written</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/spk_writer_spice_oracle.rs (inventory, evaluation, bit and byte identity, OEM epoch conversion; four planned mutation checks turn it red)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">SPICE SPK types 3 and 21 (Chebyshev, extended difference lines)</span><code class="lg-mod">spk_<wbr>writer <wbr>(SpkType::<wbr>Chebyshev3,<wbr> <wbr>SpkType::<wbr>DiffLines21,<wbr> <wbr>SpkSegment,<wbr> <wbr>InterpolatedSpk)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Writer and reader of NAIF SPICE SPK segments of type 3 (Chebyshev polynomials for position and velocity over fixed intervals, degree 0 to 27) and type 21 (extended modified difference arrays, the difference lines of JPL integrators, MAXDIM 15 to 25), from given records, in files that also hold type 9 and 13 segments; record selection and evaluation in the order of the SPICE readers. Kshana does not fit Chebyshev coefficients or produce difference lines</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0 (spkw03, spkw13 and, from the same library by its f2c symbol, spkw21; spkpvn, spkgeo), pre-registered (3f8945c7) before the code existed, the synthetic records drawn from that commit&#x27;s SHA-1: for three files (type 3 degrees 0, 12 and 27 with 3 to 150 records; type 21 MAXDIM 15, 20 and 25 with 1 to 250 lines, zero to two directory entries and a centre chain; type 3, 21 and 13 in one file with comments) SPICE&#x27;s inventory of Kshana&#x27;s kernels is exact, Kshana&#x27;s kernels are byte-identical to the ones SPICE writes from the same records, and spkpvn and spkgeo equal Kshana&#x27;s reader bit for bit on Kshana&#x27;s and on SPICE&#x27;s files (48 876 components each way, bar 1e-12 relative). Synthetic records: nothing about physical accuracy; real JPL-produced type 21 kernels are not in the case set</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/spk_3_21_spice_oracle.rs (inventory, evaluation both ways, byte identity); spk_writer::tests</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">CCSDS Attitude Data Messages (AEM, APM)</span><code class="lg-mod">adm <wbr>(AemMessage,<wbr> <wbr>ApmMessage,<wbr> <wbr>quaternion_<wbr>a_<wbr>to_<wbr>b),<wbr> <wbr>ndm_<wbr>xml</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Writer and reader of the CCSDS 504.0-B-2 Attitude Data Messages in KVN and NDM/XML: AEM version 2.0 (and 1.0 in KVN, with its ATTITUDE_DIR, QUATERNION_TYPE and numeric rotation sequences) with quaternion, Euler-angle and spin attitude types and their derivative, angular-velocity and nutation variants, several segments; APM version 2.0 with quaternion, Euler, angular-velocity, spin, inertia and maneuver blocks; the A-to-B quaternion of a record (504.0-B-2 Annex F)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 13.1.8 (Apache-2.0) parsers and writers, run as a separate program (xval/adm-orekit), pre-registered (2a4c44b3) before any comparison: Orekit parses every Kshana-written message (14 AEM KVN, 11 AEM XML, 4 APM KVN, 4 APM XML) and every value is inside its bar (quaternions 1e-14, angles 1e-9 deg; worst 0.054 of a bar); Kshana parses the 31 messages Orekit writes from the same inputs inside every bar; the rotation of each of 135 quaternion, Euler and spin records equals AemSegment.getAngularCoordinates within 1e-12 (worst 0.0071 of the bar). All 15 Kshana XML messages validate against the NDM/XML 4.0.0 schema (data-gated, fetched with pinned SHA-256). Finding: Orekit&#x27;s APM writer refuses a maneuver without MAN_DELTA_MASS (optional in the standard), so APM maneuvers are covered in the Kshana-writes direction only. Outside the case set: AEM 1.0 */RATE types and XML, repeated APM blocks</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/adm_orekit_oracle.rs (L1 Orekit reads 33 Kshana files, L2 Kshana reads 31 Orekit files, L3 135 record rotations; findings pinned); adm::tests; ndm_xml::tests</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">CCSDS Orbit Data Messages (OPM, OMM, OCM)</span><code class="lg-mod">odm <wbr>(OpmMessage,<wbr> <wbr>OmmMessage,<wbr> <wbr>OcmMessage,<wbr> <wbr>Section,<wbr> <wbr>OcmBlock),<wbr> <wbr>ndm_<wbr>xml</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Writer and reader of the CCSDS 502.0-B-3 Orbit Data Messages in KVN and NDM/XML: OPM 3.0 and 2.0 (state vector, osculating Keplerian elements, spacecraft parameters, covariance, maneuvers, user-defined parameters), OMM 3.0 and 2.0 (mean elements by mean motion or semi-major axis, TLE-related parameters with BSTAR or BTERM and AGOM, covariance), and OCM 3.0 with trajectory (CARTP, CARTPV, CARTPVA; absolute or relative epochs), physical-properties, covariance (CARTP, CARTPV in LTM order), maneuver and user-defined blocks; a full-precision OMM from an SGP4 element set beside the unchanged OmmFile::to_omm_kvn</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 13.1.8 (Apache-2.0) OPM, OMM and OCM parsers and writers, run as a separate program (xval/odm-orekit), pre-registered (8efea7c1) before any comparison: 27 of the 30 Kshana-written messages (6 OPM, 5 OMM and 5 OCM cases in KVN, and XML for version 3.0) parse in Orekit with every value within 1e-14 relative and every epoch within 1e-9 s (904 values, worst 0.021 of a bar), and Kshana reads all 25 messages Orekit writes back out inside the same bars (858 values, worst 0.020); all 14 Kshana XML messages validate against the NDM/XML 4.0.0 schema (data-gated). Findings, pinned, narrowing the claim: Orekit requires ELEMENT_SET_NO (optional in 502.0-B-3), refuses COMMENT in XML user-defined parameters (the schema allows it), will not write BTERM without BSTAR (so BTERM and AGOM are covered in the Kshana-writes direction only), and adds OCM relative epochs as SI seconds, so under TDB they differ from the TDB calendar reading by about 1.9e-10 s/s (UTC, TAI and TT agree). The legacy OmmFile::to_omm_kvn output, which wraps OMM metadata in META_START/META_STOP, is refused by Orekit (recorded). Out of scope: OCM PERT and OD blocks, non-Cartesian element sets, other covariance orderings</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/odm_orekit_oracle.rs (L1 Orekit reads 30 Kshana files, L2 Kshana reads 25 Orekit files; findings pinned and asserted to recur); odm::tests</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">3-DOF attitude &amp; pointing error budget (AOCS)</span><code class="lg-mod">attitude_<wbr>budget</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Gravity-gradient worst-case disturbance torque ((3/2)(μ/R³)ΔI) as the peak over attitude (the validated claim), plus an RSS pointing-error budget over named 1σ contributors with the dominant term (a quadrature sum of caller-supplied numbers, outside the validated claim). Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>attitude-budget</code> scenario kind (scenarios/attitude-budget.toml)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Basilisk 2.9.1 GravityGradientEffector (AVSLab, ISC), an independent C++ implementation of the full-tensor gravity-gradient torque: Kshana&#x27;s peak (3/2)(μ/R³)ΔI equals Basilisk&#x27;s torque at the 45 deg attitude within 1e-12 relative (worst 2.1e-15) over 5 altitudes and 8 inertia tensors, and bounds Basilisk&#x27;s torque over 2000 random attitudes per case; tolerance fixed before the comparison. Validated claim: the gravity-gradient torque peak. The RSS pointing budget is a quadrature sum of caller-supplied numbers with no external truth and stays outside it; the hand-coded Hipparchus full-tensor maximisation remains as supporting self-consistency. No control-loop/6-DoF/flexible-mode simulation</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>attitude_budget::tests (GG torque vanishes for a symmetric body, grows lower-down, linear in ΔI, RSS quadrature sum, variance-fractions-sum-to-1); tests/attitude_gg_torque_reference.rs (20 cases vs an independent…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Ground-station pass prediction (ground segment)</span><code class="lg-mod">passes</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Time-domain visibility passes (AOS/TCA/LOS, max elevation, duration) of an engine propagator over a station above an elevation mask, with interpolated rise/set crossings and total access: passes::predict_passes, the geometric scheduler (no refraction or light time). The <code>passes</code> scenario kind runs the apparent predictor of the row &#x27;Apparent ground-station pass prediction with refraction and light time&#x27;</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) + Hipparchus 3.1 — an independent flight-dynamics library: ElevationDetector (Brent root-finder) + EventsLogger over an ITRF ephemeris, station as a WGS-84 TopocentricFrame. AOS/LOS/max-elevation/pass-count/total-access matched on identical orbit+station+mask+window (committed fixture; driver xval/orekit-passes)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>passes::tests (interp-cross linear + degenerate-safe, polar→mid-lat passes, higher mask ⇒ fewer-or-equal); tests/ground_station_pass_prediction_reference.rs (22 scenarios / 129 passes vs Orekit 12.2 ElevationDetector…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Apparent ground-station pass prediction with refraction and light time</span><code class="lg-mod">passes <wbr>(predict_<wbr>passes_<wbr>apparent,<wbr> <wbr>apparent_<wbr>look_<wbr>angles,<wbr> <wbr>ApparentOptions,<wbr> <wbr>p834_<wbr>tau_<wbr>s_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>p834_<wbr>tau_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>p834_<wbr>theta_<wbr>m_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>p834_<wbr>apparent_<wbr>elevation_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>PassesScenario)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Visibility passes (AOS, TCA, LOS, maximum apparent elevation, duration) of an SGP4 element set over a WGS-84 station on the validated Earth-orbit path (IAU 2006/2000A, optional Earth orientation parameters), with ITU-R P.834-9 tropospheric refraction (equations 9 to 14, the Recommendation&#x27;s printed coefficients) and the downlink light time, each switchable; crossings solved by bisection to 1e-7 s and the culmination by golden-section search. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>passes</code> scenario kind (scenarios/passes.toml), both corrections on by default</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 13.1.8 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) with Hipparchus 4.0.3: TLEPropagator on the same element sets in a TEME Orekit builds to the stated definition (its IERS 2010 TOD rotated by GAST - GMST), ElevationDetector and ElevationExtremumDetector with and without ITURP834AtmosphericRefraction, and AngularAzEl with AngularRadioRefractionModifier for the light-time case, crossings and maximum solved by Hipparchus; 60 cases (four orbits, five sea-level stations, masks 0, 5 and 10 deg, 24 hours). Bars fixed before the run (pre-registered 896f3c2e, leg 3 of d92aef9b). Light time ignored (9777 failures) or the P.834 constant 1.728 changed to 1.828 (7883 failures) turns the test red. Scope: stations at sea level; Orekit&#x27;s P.834 h*theta0^2 coefficient is 0.011380 where the Recommendation prints 0.01380 (pinned), so heights above sea level are not compared. Round 1, against Orekit&#x27;s default IERS 1996 TEME, held every pass bar but missed the direction bar by the 72 mas TEME convention gap (pinned)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/pass_predictor_on_path_orekit_oracle.rs (60 cases, 1210 passes: AOS/LOS within 3.4e-6 s, bar 5 ms; maximum elevation within 5.5e-7 deg, bar 1e-3 deg; 8979 apparent directions within 8.8e-7 deg, bar 1e-4 deg)…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">DVB-S2 adaptive coding and modulation along a LEO downlink pass</span><code class="lg-mod">contact_<wbr>schedule <wbr>(es_<wbr>n0_<wbr>db,<wbr> <wbr>select_<wbr>modcod,<wbr> <wbr>fspl_<wbr>db,<wbr> <wbr>atmospheric_<wbr>loss_<wbr>db,<wbr> <wbr>ccm_<wbr>modcod,<wbr> <wbr>pass_<wbr>capacity,<wbr> <wbr>AcmLinkScenario); <wbr>passes::<wbr>predict_<wbr>passes_<wbr>apparent</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Downlink Es/N0 along each pass of one satellite over one station (EIRP, free-space loss, cosecant-law atmospheric loss, other losses, G/T, Boltzmann&#x27;s constant, symbol rate), the most efficient DVB-S2 MODCOD that clears its ETSI EN 302 307-1 V1.4.1 Table 13 quasi-error-free threshold plus an implementation margin at each step, the pass&#x27;s ACM data volume, and the constant-coding baseline that closes at the mask and the longest range. Runnable as the <code>acm-link</code> scenario kind. Illustrative inputs; MODELLED.</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Internal consistency: the spectral efficiency computed in closed form from Table 5a K_bch and the frame lengths agrees with the efficiencies Table 13 prints (a transcription check on the threshold table); the link-budget pieces agree with their closed forms; the selection is monotone in Es/N0 and honours the margin; ACM moves at least the constant-coding volume. The pass geometry comes from the separately VALIDATED apparent pass predictor. WHAT IS NOT: no external tool or measured link has been compared; the thresholds are the standard&#x27;s ideal AWGN values, not a modem&#x27;s; rain fade, pointing loss along the pass and pilots are not modelled.</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>contact_schedule::tests::modcod_efficiency_closed_form_matches_the_printed_table; contact_schedule::tests::modcod_selection_is_monotone_and_honours_the_margin…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Multi-satellite, multi-station ground-contact scheduling with conflict resolution</span><code class="lg-mod">contact_<wbr>schedule <wbr>(conflicts,<wbr> <wbr>objective,<wbr> <wbr>delivered,<wbr> <wbr>schedule_<wbr>chronological,<wbr> <wbr>schedule_<wbr>greedy,<wbr> <wbr>schedule_<wbr>optimal,<wbr> <wbr>ContactScheduleScenario)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Candidate contacts from every satellite-station pass with its ACM data volume; conflicts where one antenna would serve two satellites inside its turnaround or one satellite would downlink to two stations at once; a priority-weighted delivery objective capped at each satellite&#x27;s backlog; a lazy marginal-value greedy plan, a first-come baseline and an exact branch and bound on up to 26 candidates, with the greedy&#x27;s optimality gap reported. Runnable as the <code>contact-schedule</code> scenario kind. Illustrative inputs; MODELLED.</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Internal consistency: the exact branch and bound agrees with brute-force enumeration on small random sets, every plan is checked feasible against the conflict rules, and the greedy never beats the exact optimum. WHAT IS NOT: no external scheduler or operational plan has been compared; contacts are taken whole (no splitting), the backlog is fixed at the window start, slew time beyond the fixed turnaround and onboard storage are not modelled.</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>contact_schedule::tests::conflicts_cover_turnaround_and_one_station_per_satellite; contact_schedule::tests::greedy_is_feasible_and_the_exact_solver_beats_or_ties_it…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">One-way link budget (comm