+ModelledSpace RF geolocation bounds for a surface emitter seen from low Earth orbit: one satellite, two- and three-satellite formations, and several passes fusedspace_geoloc
+
+Capability
+
+space_geoloc: the constrained Cramer-Rao bound (the emitter on the WGS 84 ellipsoid) of a static surface emitter from one satellite's frequency of arrival over a pass (S1), from a two- or three-satellite formation's time and frequency differences (F2, F3), and from several passes fused (MP), with the noise per measurement from a free-space link budget; space_geoloc::ccrlb_with_receiver_errors adds receiver position and velocity errors as Gaussian nuisance parameters (Cao et al. 2015), and space_geoloc::solve_f3 is a Gauss-Newton estimator for F3
+
+Oracle
+
+Bars pre-registered in docs/design/evidence/m1-space-rf-geolocation/ (results a41eb6d06). All MET: B1, an independent numpy recomputation (xval/m1-geolocation/oracle.py, no shared code) over 60 cases (S1, F2, F3 x P1-P10 x AIS, a 30 dBm jammer): worst relative difference 3.0e-10 and worst orientation 3.0e-11 rad (bars 1e-6), every pass identical; B2, the published case of Cao et al., Chin. J. Aeronaut. 2015, Fig. 1 (25 plot readings, bar 20 %): worst +5.5 % (at a receiver-location error of 0.75 km), 22 of 25 within 1 %; B3, the F3 estimator against the bound (AIS, P1-P10, 1000 trials each, bar 0.9 to 1.25 x DRMS and 99 % converged): ratios 0.979 to 1.019, 1000 of 1000 converged at every point; B4, the fused information equals the sum of the per-pass information to 6.7e-16 relative and DRMS never rises with another pass; B5 additivity. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: no flown system and no real RF data; no clock or orbit-knowledge error, multipath, AIS slot collisions seen from orbit, ionosphere at VHF or emitter motion; the link budgets are representative, not measured, and the radar classes isotropic (an upper bound), so the bounds are optimistic
+
